"""Original PE Studies lesson explanations and sport scenarios. Teaching headings are authored."""
LESSONS=[]
def L(u,slug,title,area,theory,question,answer,practice,solution,limit,diagram):
 LESSONS.append(dict(unit=u,slug=slug,title=title,area=area,theory=theory.split('||'),question=question,answer=answer.split('||'),practice=practice,solution=solution.split('||'),limit=limit,diagram=diagram))
L(1,'bones','Locate the bones that support movement','Functional anatomy',
'''The skeleton provides support, protects organs and supplies attachment sites for muscles. A bone does not actively shorten to create a movement. Muscular force transmitted through tendons turns bones around joints. Learn locations together with a sporting action rather than memorising a disconnected list. The humerus forms the upper arm; radius and ulna form the forearm.||The femur is the thigh bone, the patella lies at the front of the knee, and tibia and fibula form the lower leg. The pelvis connects trunk and lower limbs. Sternum and ribs protect the thorax. Carpals form the wrist, metacarpals the palm and phalanges the fingers. Tarsals form the rear and mid foot, metatarsals the forefoot and phalanges the toes.||In a basketball pass, the humerus and forearm bones move while the trunk supplies a stable base. Naming a bone alone does not identify the joint action or the muscle producing it. When a drawing is simplified, use anatomical position as the reference: standing upright, palms facing forward. Radius is on the thumb side in that position.||Practise tracing the sequence from pelvis through femur, tibia and tarsals to the toes, then from humerus to the fingers. Use location plus function in an explanation. Distinguish a bone from a muscle, ligament or tendon. Ligaments connect bones at joints; tendons transmit muscle force to bone. This lesson identifies structures, not injuries.''',
'A student labels a basketball player’s upper arm radius and the lower leg femur. Correct both labels and name the two forearm bones.',
'Upper arm: humerus, not radius.||Lower leg: tibia and fibula, not femur; femur is in the thigh.||The forearm contains radius and ulna. Radius is on the thumb side in anatomical position.',
'Put these structures in order from wrist to fingertip and ankle region to toe: phalanges, metatarsals, carpals, tarsals, metacarpals. Explain how bones contribute to a pass.',
'Wrist to fingertip: carpals → metacarpals → phalanges.||Ankle region to toes: tarsals → metatarsals → phalanges.||Muscles pull through tendons to rotate bones around joints; bones provide support and leverage. They do not contract themselves.',
'A schematic shows relationships, not a diagnostic anatomical image.','skeleton')
L(1,'muscles-upper','Upper-body muscles and attachments','Functional anatomy',
'''Skeletal muscles pull on attachments when they develop tension. Biceps brachii is associated with elbow flexion; triceps brachii with elbow extension. Deltoid contributes to shoulder abduction, pectorals to movements bringing the arm across the body, and latissimus dorsi to shoulder extension and adduction. Actions depend on which fibres act and the starting position.||Trapezius acts on the shoulder girdle rather than simply bending the elbow. Abdominal muscles help control the trunk. Origin conventionally names the less mobile attachment and insertion the more mobile one. During a closed-chain action such as a chin-up, which attachment moves most can change. These labels do not mean that one attachment is always motionless.||An antagonist produces an opposing joint action to an agonist. During a simple elbow-flexion action, biceps contributes as an agonist while triceps opposes flexion. Antagonists can co-contract to stabilise a joint; they do not always completely switch off. A muscle can perform different roles in different movements. Explain the role in the actual phase observed.||A push-up rising phase includes elbow extension by triceps and shoulder actions involving pectorals. A single-muscle explanation misses the coordinated nature of the task. Identify the joint, the direction of motion and the relevant muscle action before describing attachments. Do not infer exact muscle activity or force solely from an external photograph.''',
'Explain why triceps can be an agonist in a chest pass but an antagonist during an elbow curl.',
'The role depends on the joint action and movement phase, not a permanent label.||During elbow extension in the pass, triceps produces the action and acts as an agonist.||During elbow flexion in a curl, biceps contributes to flexion and triceps produces the opposing extension action. Co-contraction may also stabilise the joint.',
'A student says insertion always stays still and muscles push bones. Correct the claim and identify an upper-body muscle relevant to shoulder abduction.',
'Muscles develop pulling tension through attachments; they do not push bones away.||Insertion conventionally refers to the more mobile attachment, but actual movement depends on whether the action is open or closed chain.||Deltoid contributes to shoulder abduction; state the joint and direction rather than claiming it produces all shoulder movement alone.',
'Visible motion does not prove which individual fibres are active.','antagonists')
L(1,'muscles-lower','Lower-body muscles in running and jumping','Functional anatomy',
'''Quadriceps are at the front of the thigh and contribute to knee extension. Hamstrings lie posteriorly and contribute to knee flexion and hip extension. Gluteus maximus contributes to hip extension; hip flexors contribute to hip flexion. Adductors draw the thigh towards the midline. Some muscles cross more than one joint, so their role depends on both joint positions.||Gastrocnemius and soleus contribute to plantarflexion at the ankle. Gastrocnemius also crosses the knee; soleus does not. Tibialis anterior contributes to dorsiflexion, lifting the forefoot towards the shin. During a jump, coordinated hip and knee extension and ankle plantarflexion contribute to propulsion. This is an action sequence, not proof that each muscle reaches maximum force simultaneously.||During landing, quadriceps can be active while lengthening to control knee flexion. The joint moves into flexion even though knee extensors develop force. This eccentric action is different from claiming the muscle must shorten whenever it works. Isometric tension can also stabilise a position with little change in joint angle.||Analyse take-off and landing as different phases. State a muscle, the joint it influences, the action and whether the muscle mainly shortens, lengthens under load or holds approximately steady. Use the whole chain from trunk through hip, knee and ankle. Practical technique should be developed with the teacher; text alone cannot establish safe landing proficiency.''',
'Compare quadriceps action in upward jump propulsion and controlled landing.',
'During propulsion the knee extends; quadriceps can shorten concentrically to contribute to extension.||During a controlled landing the knee flexes while quadriceps develops tension and lengthens eccentrically.||The muscle controls deceleration; active does not always mean shortening. Hip and ankle actions also contribute.',
'Identify the ankle action and relevant muscles when a runner pushes off, then when the forefoot is raised. Name a hip extensor and hip flexor group.',
'Push-off involves plantarflexion, with gastrocnemius and soleus contributing.||Raising the forefoot involves dorsiflexion, with tibialis anterior contributing.||Gluteus maximus contributes to hip extension; hip flexors contribute to hip flexion. Roles vary with movement phase and joint positions.',
'These examples explain actions; they are not an exercise prescription.','joint-actions')
L(1,'properties','Muscle properties and opposing pairs','Functional anatomy',
'''Excitability is the ability of muscle tissue to respond to stimulation. Contractility is the ability to develop tension and shorten when conditions allow. Extensibility is the ability to be stretched, and elasticity is the tendency to return towards original length after the stretching force is removed. These properties describe tissue behaviour rather than four separate training methods.||A nerve signal can initiate activation, but the resulting joint movement depends on the external load, muscle length and coordination. In an isometric action the muscle develops force without an appreciable change in overall muscle length. Contractility therefore does not imply that every active muscle visibly shortens in every task.||Antagonistic pairs create opposing actions around a joint. Biceps and triceps are a familiar elbow example; quadriceps and hamstrings provide a knee example. During stabilisation both sides may activate. Tendons transmit tension, while ligaments contribute passive joint restraint. Mixing these structures leads to an incorrect explanation of how the movement is produced.||Explain a sport action in an ordered chain: stimulation leads to muscle tension, that tension pulls through an attachment, and the resulting torque can move or stabilise a joint. Link each property to the correct part of the chain. Elasticity is not the same as being extremely flexible, and excitability does not mean emotional excitement.''',
'An athlete holds an elbow angle steady against a load. Does absence of visible shortening show the muscle is inactive? Explain.',
'No. An isometric action can develop tension with little overall length change.||Excitability permits response to a stimulus and contractility permits force development.||The muscle’s torque can balance the external torque, maintaining the angle; opposing muscles may also contribute to stability.',
'Match ability to respond, develop tension, be stretched and return towards original length to the four properties. Explain one antagonist pair.',
'Respond: excitability; develop tension: contractility; be stretched: extensibility; return: elasticity.||At the elbow, biceps contributes to flexion and triceps to extension.||Their agonist and antagonist roles depend on the task; co-contraction can stabilise the joint.',
'Overall muscle length and individual fibre behaviour are not always identical.','antagonists')
L(1,'joint-actions','Name joint actions precisely','Functional anatomy',
'''Flexion generally reduces the angle between adjoining bones and extension increases it. Abduction moves away from the body’s midline, while adduction moves towards it. Rotation turns a segment about its long axis. Circumduction combines actions so the distal end traces a circle; it is not simply another word for rotation. Always identify the joint as well as the action.||Pronation and supination describe forearm rotation relative to anatomical position. With the elbow held at the side, supination turns the palm towards an upward orientation and pronation turns it towards downward. Dorsiflexion brings the top of the foot towards the shin, while plantarflexion points the foot away. These terms cannot be assigned from a single ambiguous camera view without context.||A shoulder may flex while the elbow extends during part of a pass. Saying the whole arm extends blurs two different joint actions. A throw also involves trunk rotation and coordinated lower-limb actions. Describing the whole skill requires its phases and relevant joints, not one global movement label.||Use a before-and-after description to decide the direction. Ask which segment moves relative to which, locate the reference position and check the plane. A motion can combine several actions. The named action describes movement, not necessarily which muscle is shortening; controlling an external load may require eccentric work by an opposing action group.''',
'During a pass the elbow straightens while the upper arm moves forward at the shoulder. Name both actions and explain why one whole-arm label is inadequate.',
'Elbow straightening is extension.||Forward elevation of the upper arm in this stated sagittal movement is shoulder flexion.||Different joints perform different actions, so a single arm-extension label loses the distinction.',
'Distinguish forearm pronation from shoulder rotation, and circumduction from plantarflexion. Give an adduction example.',
'Pronation is forearm rotation turning the palm from the supinated reference towards the pronated orientation. Shoulder rotation concerns the humerus about its long axis.||Circumduction traces a circular path through combined joint actions; plantarflexion points the foot away from the shin.||Bringing an abducted arm back towards the trunk is shoulder adduction.',
'Direction must be specified relative to anatomical position and the joint.','joint-actions')
L(1,'circulation','Trace circulation through heart and vessels','Exercise physiology',
'''The circulatory system transports oxygen, nutrients and other materials and helps distribute heat. The right side of the heart pumps blood towards the lungs; the left side pumps it to the body. Arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins return it. Direction, rather than oxygen content, defines these vessel categories.||Pulmonary arteries carry relatively deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle towards the lungs. Pulmonary veins return oxygenated blood to the left atrium. Systemic arteries then deliver blood from the left ventricle to tissues. Capillaries provide thin exchange surfaces between blood and tissues; haemoglobin in red blood cells carries much of the blood’s oxygen.||During exercise, active muscles need more oxygen delivery and waste transport. Increased cardiac output and changed distribution support these demands. Blood flow to skin may also rise to assist heat loss. It is inaccurate to say all blood goes to muscle or that blood simply becomes completely oxygen-free after one circuit.||Trace a red blood cell through the full circuit: right heart, lungs, left heart, body and back to the right heart. Separate the pump from the exchange site. Explain oxygen loading at pulmonary capillaries and unloading at systemic tissues through gradients. Do not infer an athlete’s heart health from a classroom heart-rate value alone.''',
'Correct: all arteries carry oxygenated blood. Trace blood from the right ventricle to the left ventricle.',
'Arteries are defined by carrying blood away from the heart.||Right ventricle → pulmonary artery → lung capillaries → pulmonary veins → left atrium → left ventricle.||Pulmonary arteries carry relatively deoxygenated blood and pulmonary veins oxygenated blood.',
'Explain how a capillary differs functionally from an artery and why muscles need increased circulation during exercise.',
'An artery carries blood away from the heart; capillaries form exchange networks with thin walls and close tissue contact.||Active muscle requires increased oxygen and substrate delivery and removal of metabolic products.||Higher cardiac output and redistribution support the demand; skin circulation can also contribute to heat regulation.',
'Blood oxygen content is graded, not simply present or absent.','circulation')
L(1,'breathing','Ventilation and gas exchange','Exercise physiology',
'''Ventilation moves air into and out of lungs; gas exchange moves gases across respiratory surfaces. During quiet inhalation, the diaphragm contracts and descends. Thoracic volume rises and pressure inside the lungs falls below atmospheric pressure, drawing air inward. This sequence is mechanical and does not mean the lungs actively suck by muscular contraction of lung tissue.||During quiet exhalation the diaphragm relaxes and elastic recoil reduces lung volume. Pressure rises relative to the atmosphere and air flows outward. Forceful exhalation during exercise can involve additional muscles. In the alveoli, oxygen and carbon dioxide diffuse down partial-pressure gradients across a thin exchange surface into or out of adjacent capillary blood.||Breathing rate and tidal volume generally rise with exercise intensity, increasing minute ventilation. Minute ventilation equals breathing frequency multiplied by tidal volume. This total airflow is not the same as oxygen consumed: some air occupies conducting passages and gas exchange depends on perfusion and gradients as well as ventilation.||Explain movement of air with volume, pressure and direction in that order. Explain gas exchange separately with surface area, short diffusion distance and gradients. A deeper breath is not proof of greater aerobic fitness, and exercise adaptations should not be explained as adult lungs simply growing larger.''',
'Breathing frequency is 24 breaths/min and tidal volume 1.5 L/breath. Calculate minute ventilation and explain quiet inhalation.',
'24 × 1.5 = 36 L/min.||Diaphragm contracts and descends, increasing thoracic volume.||Pressure falls relative to atmospheric pressure, so air moves inward; ventilation is distinct from oxygen uptake.',
'Compare quiet exhalation with inhalation and explain why 36 L/min airflow is not 36 L/min oxygen consumption.',
'Quiet exhalation involves diaphragm relaxation and elastic recoil; volume falls, pressure rises and air flows outward.||Inhalation involves diaphragm contraction, increased volume and reduced pressure.||Air contains mixed gases, some airflow ventilates conducting spaces, and only part of delivered oxygen is taken up. Gas exchange also depends on perfusion and gradients.',
'This is normal respiratory physiology, not assessment of breathing symptoms.','ventilation')
L(1,'acute','Acute exercise responses','Exercise physiology',
'''An acute response occurs during or shortly after a bout of exercise. Heart rate usually rises, stroke volume often rises and cardiac output increases to support working tissues. Systolic blood pressure generally rises in dynamic exercise, while the diastolic response depends on the task and conditions. Avoid the blanket statement that both pressures always increase by the same amount.||Cardiac output is heart rate multiplied by stroke volume. With stroke volume in millilitres per beat, the product is millilitres per minute; divide by 1000 for litres per minute. Respiratory rate and tidal volume support increased ventilation. Perspiration and changes in skin blood flow help manage heat but depend on temperature, humidity and the person.||Blood is redistributed towards active muscles and, when needed, skin. Other organ flow changes vary; it does not cease altogether. The immediate response is different from the long-term adaptation obtained from repeated training. A high heart rate in one fictional task cannot by itself diagnose dehydration, fitness level or illness.||Interpret data using the activity intensity, environment and measurement timing. State a supported pattern, calculate where appropriate and explain the physiological mechanism. Separate a description, such as heart rate increased, from its functional explanation, such as delivering a greater volume of blood per minute to meet demand.''',
'Heart rate rises from 60 to 150 beats/min and stroke volume from 80 to 120 mL/beat. Calculate cardiac output at both points.',
'Rest: 60 × 80 = 4800 mL/min = 4.8 L/min.||Exercise: 150 × 120 = 18000 mL/min = 18 L/min.||Output increases by 13.2 L/min, supporting greater oxygen transport and substrate delivery. Both factors contribute.',
'A player’s breathing rate and perspiration rise in a warm match. Explain two different functions and one limit on interpreting these observations.',
'Increased ventilation supports gas exchange as metabolic demand increases.||Sweat evaporation can remove heat; sweating alone is not proof that all sweat evaporates.||Responses depend on workload and environment. These observations alone do not establish hydration status, fitness or a medical diagnosis.',
'All values in the worked data are invented for learning.','cardiac-output')
L(1,'chronic','Chronic cardiovascular and respiratory adaptations','Exercise physiology',
'''Chronic adaptations develop through repeated training over time. Endurance training can increase stroke volume through structural and functional cardiac adaptations and greater blood volume. Resting heart rate may fall because an adequate resting cardiac output can be supplied with fewer beats. Cardiac hypertrophy in this context means a training-related adaptation, not that every enlarged heart is healthy.||Greater capillary supply and oxidative capacity in trained muscle support oxygen delivery and use. Maximal oxygen uptake can improve, although response varies with initial fitness, training and other factors. Total haemoglobin mass and blood volume can change; this should not be confused with assuming haemoglobin concentration must always increase because concentration also depends on plasma volume.||Ventilatory efficiency and respiratory-muscle function can improve. At the same submaximal workload a trained athlete may experience lower relative strain. Do not explain this as the lungs necessarily becoming anatomically much bigger. Blood-pressure adaptations depend on the person and baseline values; a training programme is not a guarantee of an identical pressure change for everyone.||To identify adaptation, compare comparable measurements before and after a period of training. Control relative workload, environmental conditions and timing. A heart-rate difference measured in different activities is not enough. Explain the link between a changed structure or function and a performance demand while acknowledging other possible influences.''',
'Before training: resting HR 72 and SV 70 mL. Afterwards: HR 60 and SV 84 mL. Compare resting cardiac output.',
'Before: 72 × 70 = 5040 mL/min.||After: 60 × 84 = 5040 mL/min.||Output is unchanged at 5.04 L/min in this fictional dataset; greater SV permits fewer beats. Lower HR does not imply less necessary resting blood delivery.',
'A student claims aerobic training guarantees larger lungs and higher haemoglobin concentration. Replace this with a qualified explanation of two adaptations.',
'Improved stroke volume and blood volume can support circulation; resting heart rate may decrease.||Muscle capillarisation and oxidative capacity can support oxygen use. Ventilatory function may become more efficient without requiring greatly enlarged adult lungs.||Haemoglobin mass and concentration differ; plasma-volume changes influence concentration. Individual adaptation varies.',
'Training responses vary; the lesson does not interpret clinical results.','cardiac-output')
L(1,'nutrients','Macronutrients as exercise fuels and building materials','Exercise physiology',
'''Carbohydrate, fat and protein are macronutrients with different roles. Carbohydrate supplies glucose and stored glycogen, supporting high-rate energy provision as well as aerobic metabolism. Fat stores substantial energy and is an important aerobic fuel. Protein provides amino acids for tissue repair and many functional molecules; its main sporting role is not simply being the preferred fuel for every sprint.||The body continually resynthesises ATP, the immediate energy currency used by contracting muscle. Food nutrients are not directly interchangeable with ATP at the contractile machinery. Their oxidation and other metabolic pathways contribute to replenishing ATP. Relative carbohydrate and fat contribution changes with intensity, duration, diet and training; both can contribute during the same activity.||A prolonged match involves repeated intense efforts and lower-intensity periods. Stored carbohydrate can be important for maintaining repeated work, while adequate protein supports recovery and adaptation. Fat is not useless in sport, and removing a nutrient group is not a sound deduction from a simplified energy-system table. Nutrient requirements are individual.||For an educational response, identify the demand, the nutrient’s role and timing considerations, without prescribing a personal diet. Distinguish fuel availability from muscle repair. Examine a claim such as protein powder automatically improves performance by asking whether it addresses a real dietary need and whether the wider training and eating pattern is appropriate.''',
'Explain why a repeated-sprint player needs more than the claim protein builds muscle.',
'Carbohydrate availability supports repeated high-rate ATP resynthesis and glycogen stores.||Fat contributes aerobic fuel, especially during lower-intensity work; all systems overlap.||Protein supplies amino acids for repair and adaptation, but extra protein is not a substitute for adequate energy, training and recovery.',
'Critique: fat supplies no energy in exercise and carbohydrate can be replaced entirely by a protein shake.',
'Fat contributes energy through aerobic metabolism. Its relative contribution depends on intensity and duration.||Carbohydrate supports glucose and glycogen availability, particularly important during demanding repeated efforts.||Protein has repair and functional roles; one shake is not evidence of a complete diet or a universal replacement for carbohydrate.',
'Nutrition examples teach mechanisms, not personal meal plans.','energy')
L(1,'energy-systems','Three overlapping energy systems','Exercise physiology',
'''ATP must be resynthesised as muscle uses it. The ATP–CP system uses phosphocreatine to regenerate ATP rapidly and has limited capacity. Anaerobic glycolysis supplies ATP from carbohydrate without requiring oxygen directly in those steps. The syllabus also uses the label lactic acid system. Aerobic metabolism can use carbohydrate and fat with a comparatively large capacity.||All three systems contribute from the beginning of activity. Dominance depends on intensity, duration, recovery and training rather than a timer that turns one system off and another on. A short maximal acceleration often relies heavily on ATP–CP, but that does not make aerobic contribution zero. Aerobic processes also support recovery between repeated efforts.||Lactate is a metabolic product and can be used as fuel. It is not simply a waste substance that independently explains all fatigue or delayed-onset muscle soreness. The rate of glycolytic ATP provision, associated metabolic changes and limits across systems affect performance. Explain energy supply separately from over-simplified claims about one fatigue chemical.||Compare rate and capacity: rapid supply is useful for explosive efforts, while large capacity supports sustained work. Analyse a whole sporting sequence rather than assigning an entire sport to one system. A rally, pause and next rally have different demands. Recovery duration changes phosphocreatine restoration and the capacity to repeat intense efforts.''',
'A tennis point lasts 6 seconds, followed by a pause and another point. Explain dominant supply and recovery without a switch model.',
'Rapid high-intensity actions may rely heavily on ATP–CP, with glycolytic and aerobic contributions also present.||Aerobic processes support recovery, including phosphocreatine restoration.||Repeated efforts and incomplete recovery can increase contributions from other pathways; no system switches completely off.',
'Compare a maximal short acceleration and a steady prolonged run. Correct: lactate causes all fatigue and next-day soreness.',
'Acceleration has a high ATP demand rate with a major ATP–CP contribution. A prolonged run generally relies predominantly on aerobic ATP resynthesis.||All systems overlap in both activities; intensity and duration affect relative contributions.||Fatigue is multifactorial, lactate can be used as fuel, and delayed soreness is not explained by retained lactic acid.',
'No fixed time boundary perfectly separates the systems.','energy')
L(1,'fitness','Health and performance fitness components','Exercise physiology',
'''Health-related components include cardiorespiratory endurance, muscular strength, muscular endurance, flexibility and body composition. Cardiorespiratory endurance supports sustained delivery and use of oxygen. Strength concerns high force capacity, whereas muscular endurance concerns repeated or sustained muscular work. Flexibility describes available joint range; body composition concerns relative body tissues, not a direct score for sporting talent.||Performance-related components include agility, balance, coordination, reaction time, speed and power. Agility involves changing direction effectively, often with perceptual demands. Balance maintains equilibrium; coordination organises movement. Reaction time is the delay between a relevant stimulus and response initiation, while speed concerns rapid movement. Power concerns rate of work or energy transfer and depends on both force and velocity.||A netball interception requires recognising a pass, initiating a response, moving quickly and coordinating the catch. Several components interact. A test samples one aspect under particular conditions and cannot represent every game demand. Fast completion of a pre-planned shuttle is not the same as reacting to unpredictable opponents.||Select a component by explaining the task demand rather than listing all eleven. Choose a repeatable assessment, explain validity and keep conditions comparable. Body-composition discussions should avoid ranking appearance or assuming that a particular physique guarantees health or performance. Use appropriately supervised school procedures for any practical measurements.''',
'A badminton player sees a shot, starts moving and changes direction to reach it. Distinguish reaction time, speed and agility.',
'Reaction time is the delay from the relevant stimulus to initiating the response.||Speed concerns how quickly the player moves.||Agility concerns effective direction change, including responding to the shot rather than merely running a fixed route.',
'Compare muscular strength and endurance, then explain why one shuttle-run score cannot describe all basketball fitness.',
'Strength concerns capacity to produce high force; endurance concerns repeated or sustained muscular work.||Basketball also demands reaction, coordination, balance, speed, power and aerobic recovery capacity.||A shuttle result samples specific conditions and may omit decision demands, skill quality and other components.',
'A fitness component is a capacity, not a complete performance prediction.','fitness')
L(1,'training-principles','FITT, overload, specificity and reversibility','Exercise physiology',
'''FITT describes frequency, intensity, time and type of training. These variables give a programme enough detail to interpret its demands. Progressive overload means increasing an appropriate training stimulus over time as adaptation occurs; it does not mean making every session maximal or increasing all variables at once. Recovery is part of adaptation.||Specificity means adaptations reflect the demands trained, including muscles, energy supply, movement patterns and skills. A programme for a distance runner and one for repeated explosive jumps may share activities but need different emphases. Reversibility means some adaptations decline when the relevant training stimulus is reduced or removed; the rate and extent vary.||A fictional programme that suddenly doubles volume and adds maximal intensity lacks a reasoned progression. Before proposing a change, identify the goal, current demands, readiness and recovery. School tasks can evaluate written plans without asking students to carry them out. Actual activity should follow the teacher’s programme and appropriate supervision.||Justify a training choice by connecting stimulus to intended adaptation and sporting demand. Include how progress would be monitored and how the plan would be adjusted when fatigue or performance signals change. A generic harder is better statement ignores both specificity and the possibility that excessive load reduces useful performance.''',
'A written plan changes from two moderate sessions to six maximal sessions immediately. Evaluate it using FITT and overload.',
'Frequency triples and intensity also rises sharply; session time and type must also be specified.||Progressive overload requires a reasoned gradual stimulus aligned to adaptation, rather than simultaneous extreme changes.||The plan lacks recovery and monitoring information. A written alternative should match the goal and allow adjustment; do not implement this fictional plan.',
'Explain why a basketball jump goal and a long-distance running goal need different specificity, and what reversibility means during a long break.',
'Jump performance emphasises explosive force, coordination and relevant movement patterns. Distance running emphasises sustained aerobic demand and endurance-specific movement.||Shared fitness work can support both, but the dominant training demands differ.||During a long reduction in relevant training, some acquired adaptations may decline; this is reversibility, with variable time course.',
'Fictional programme analysis is not an individual training prescription.','training')
L(1,'training-methods','Continuous, interval, fartlek and circuit methods','Exercise physiology',
'''Continuous training maintains activity without planned rest, usually at an intensity sustainable for the intended duration. Fartlek varies pace within ongoing activity, combining changes in effort with continued movement. Interval training alternates work and recovery periods. The lengths and intensities of both work and recovery determine which demands the session emphasises.||Short intervals can emphasise rapid high-intensity efforts, while longer intervals can support sustained aerobic work. These are broad tendencies, not guarantees from the word interval alone. A circuit organises stations with selected exercises, work duration and recovery; it can be designed for different fitness goals. The format itself does not specify an energy-system outcome.||A teacher comparing two written interval plans should consider work-to-rest structure and actual intensity, not just the number of repetitions. Active recovery differs from complete rest. Repeated maximal efforts with very short recovery cannot maintain the same output indefinitely. Decreasing skill quality or changing fatigue signals may alter the purpose of the activity.||Match method to a stated performance demand and provide enough fictional detail for analysis. Explain how the recovery period supports the next effort or creates a repeated-work challenge. A circuit of slow low-load movements is not automatically power training, and running faster for brief portions of an otherwise continuous session differs from a fully stopped interval design.''',
'Compare a continuous steady run, a run with changing pace but no stops, and repeated work bouts separated by rest.',
'Steady uninterrupted work is continuous training.||Changing pace without stopping is fartlek.||Repeated work separated by planned recovery is interval training; duration, intensity and recovery determine its emphasis.',
'Two circuits have identical station counts: one uses controlled sustained work and one brief explosive tasks with ample recovery. Explain why the same circuit label does not imply the same adaptation.',
'The exercise type and work intensity differ.||Sustained controlled work can emphasise endurance, while brief explosive work with suitable recovery can emphasise power-related demands.||Station count alone omits duration, load, movement quality and recovery; specificity requires those details.',
'Examples compare methods; actual session loads require supervision.','training')
L(1,'resistance','Resistance, flexibility and plyometrics','Exercise physiology',
'''An isometric action produces tension with little overall length change. Isotonic is commonly used in school training descriptions for dynamic resistance work, although muscle tension in ordinary free-weight movements is not literally constant throughout the motion. Concentric action shortens active muscle; eccentric action lengthens active muscle under load. Isokinetic action maintains a set angular velocity using specialised equipment.||Resistance training can target different outcomes depending on load, speed, repetition structure and recovery. Flexibility methods aim to develop or maintain range of motion. Plyometric activities exploit a rapid stretch–shortening sequence to support explosive performance; they are not simply any repeated jump and require appropriate technique and progression.||A wall hold, a controlled dynamic lift and an isokinetic device illustrate distinct action conditions. Classifying the method does not establish whether it is appropriate for a student. The load imposed, technique and supervision matter. A movement can include concentric and eccentric phases, so one label for the entire repetition may hide useful information.||Explain a method through its mechanical feature and a relevant sport demand. Avoid treating soreness as proof of a successful session. Compare written plans, observe teacher-approved demonstrations and describe phases without instructing unsupervised maximal lifting or high-impact jumping. A classroom model of a lever is a conceptual tool, not a personal lifting recommendation.''',
'Classify an elbow hold, raising and lowering a load, and a device that controls joint speed.',
'The hold is primarily isometric at the stated angle.||Raising can involve concentric muscle action and controlled lowering eccentric action; the movement is dynamic resistance work.||A device controlling angular velocity can produce isokinetic exercise.',
'Explain a stretch–shortening cycle and correct: every dynamic lift has exactly constant muscle tension.',
'A rapid active lengthening phase followed by shortening can contribute to explosive performance; timing, coordination and elastic behaviour matter.||Plyometric work uses this principle and needs appropriate supervised progression.||Ordinary dynamic resistance often changes joint torque and muscle force through its range, despite the conventional school label isotonic.',
'No loads, maximal tests or jump volumes are prescribed here.','force-length')
L(1,'space','Space, positioning and simple tactical decisions','Skills and tactics',
'''A tactic is a purposeful response to a game situation. Technique concerns how a skill is executed; a tactic concerns when, where and why it is selected. Creating space can open passing lanes and stretch a defence. Moving into space without considering the ball, opponent and teammates may instead remove support from the player who needs it.||Positioning changes with possession, field or court location and the phase of play. In an invasion game, an attacker may offer width or depth while another supports behind the ball. A defender may reduce space between the ball and goal while maintaining awareness of an opponent. These are principles to adapt, not universal formations for every sport.||Decision making depends on information: opponent position, teammate readiness, available space and risk. A low-risk pass may preserve possession, while an available scoring opportunity may justify a different choice. Evaluate the choice using the actual evidence rather than the result alone; a sound decision can fail through execution, and a poor decision can succeed by chance.||Use a simple court diagram to explain two options and the information that would favour each. Distinguish the chosen action from its expected benefit and possible limitation. Then transfer the principle to the school’s sport with teacher feedback. A written answer cannot certify skill execution in a competitive setting.''',
'A netball ball-carrier faces a marked teammate near goal and an unmarked wide teammate. Explain a possession-preserving option and a condition that might change it.',
'Passing to the unmarked wide teammate may maintain possession and create a new angle.||The decision uses defender position, passing lane and teammate readiness.||An emerging clear scoring lane or a rapidly closing wide defender could change the preferred option; outcome alone does not establish decision quality.',
'In a small-sided soccer situation, explain how one teammate offering width and another depth can help. Identify one risk of both running towards the ball.',
'Width can stretch defenders and offer a lateral passing lane; depth offers a different distance and angle of support.||If both converge, passing options and usable space may shrink.||Evaluate positions relative to opponents and phase of play; fixed instructions are not appropriate for every situation.',
'Schematic positions illustrate decisions, not a compulsory sport formation.','tactics')
L(2,'skill-types','Classify sporting skills','Motor learning and coaching',
'''Gross skills use comparatively large muscle groups and whole-body actions; fine skills emphasise precise smaller movements. Open skills occur in changing, less predictable environments, while closed skills occur in relatively stable conditions. These classifications describe continua and context, rather than assigning every sport permanently to one box.||Discrete skills have a recognisable beginning and end, such as one free throw. Serial skills link discrete elements in an ordered sequence. Continuous skills repeat without an obvious endpoint built into each cycle, such as ongoing running. A drill can combine continuous movement with discrete responses; select the specific action being classified.||A basketball free throw in a stable practice setting is relatively closed, whereas choosing and executing a pass against moving defenders is more open. The same physical technique can face different information demands when moved from isolated practice to a game. A controlled drill can improve execution while omitting perception and decision requirements.||Classify by naming the feature and providing evidence from the situation. Avoid writing closed because it happens indoors: weather exposure is only one possible environmental influence. Opponents, teammates and timing can make an indoor task open. Explain how classification should influence the practice conditions needed for transfer.''',
'Classify a stationary free-throw attempt and a pass against moving defenders by environmental predictability and temporal structure.',
'The free throw is relatively closed in the stated stable conditions and discrete.||The contested pass is relatively open because defenders and options change, and the individual pass is discrete.||Both use substantial upper-body action; classify the actual task rather than the entire sport.',
'A student says an indoor badminton rally is closed and all running is discrete. Correct both claims.',
'Indoor location does not make the rally predictable: opponent shots and positions make the task relatively open.||Ongoing running is continuous; a defined start or finish of a race does not turn each running cycle into a discrete skill.||A drill may contain several skill types, so specify the action analysed.',
'Classification is a continuum and depends on the precise task.','skill')
L(2,'learning-stages','Cognitive, associative and autonomous learning','Motor learning and coaching',
'''In the cognitive stage, a learner is developing an understanding of what to do. Performance may be inconsistent and demand considerable conscious attention. Clear demonstrations and a small number of useful cues can help. In the associative stage, the learner refines coordination and links useful feedback with changes in execution.||In the autonomous stage, familiar skill execution requires less conscious attention, allowing attention to tactical information. Automaticity is not the same as perfect performance under every condition. An experienced athlete learning a new technique or responding to an unfamiliar constraint may still require deliberate attention and renewed practice.||Fitts and Posner’s stages are a useful model, not a rigid timetable. Learners can differ across skills within the same sport. A player may execute a familiar pass automatically while learning a new serve cognitively. Assigning a stage from age alone ignores actual consistency, attentional demands and experience.||Choose coaching cues to match the learner and task. Too many instructions can overload a beginner. A more experienced learner may benefit from specific feedback about a remaining error or tactical application. Explain evidence for the stage, a suitable support strategy and what improvement would be observed, rather than simply naming the model.''',
'A novice volleyball server repeatedly asks where to stand and how to contact the ball. Identify the likely stage and an appropriate coaching response.',
'The stated uncertainty about the action and inconsistent execution suggest the cognitive stage.||Use a clear demonstration and one or two relevant cues, then allow attempts and understandable feedback.||Do not overload the learner or assume the stage solely from age.',
'An experienced basketball player executes passing with little conscious attention but struggles with a newly introduced technique. Explain why one permanent stage label is misleading.',
'Stages relate to specific skills and conditions.||A familiar pass can be relatively autonomous while the new technique requires cognitive learning.||Observe consistency and attention demand, then adapt cues and feedback to the particular skill.',
'The stage model is descriptive, not a fixed age schedule.','learning')
L(2,'cues','Visual, verbal and proprioceptive cues','Motor learning and coaching',
'''A cue directs attention towards useful information. Visual cues include demonstrations, targets and observed positions. Verbal cues are spoken instructions or brief reminders. Proprioceptive information concerns the body’s sensed position and movement, arising from receptors and integrated sensory processing rather than simply looking at oneself.||A useful cue is short, relevant and linked to the learner’s task. For a pass, a visual target can show direction, a verbal reminder can focus a key action, and sensed balance can help the learner recognise position. Different learners may use combinations; claiming everyone belongs to one fixed visual or verbal learning style is not supported by this classification.||Timing matters. A long explanation delivered during a rapid action can compete with information the athlete needs from the environment. A brief cue before practice and targeted feedback afterwards may be clearer. The coach should check whether the learner understood the cue and whether performance changes, rather than assuming the cue works because it sounds memorable.||Describe the information supplied, the movement feature it targets and how the learner can test it. Internal body sensations and externally observed outcomes provide different kinds of evidence. Neither is infallible, so a learner’s feeling of a successful movement should be compared with observation or task outcome.''',
'A coach demonstrates a serve, says contact in front, and asks the learner to notice weight shifting. Classify the three cue sources.',
'The demonstration is a visual cue.||The spoken phrase is verbal.||Noticing sensed weight shift uses proprioceptive information; it is distinct from watching a video.',
'Explain why giving six shouted instructions during a fast rally may be less useful than one cue before practice and feedback afterwards.',
'Too many instructions can overload attention and compete with perception of the rally.||A focused pre-practice cue can establish the intended feature.||Feedback afterwards can link the attempt to an observed result; suitability depends on learner and task rather than one universal rule.',
'Cues are information sources, not fixed learner personality types.','processing')
L(2,'processing','Information processing in decisions','Motor learning and coaching',
'''An information-processing model describes input, decision processes, output and feedback. Input includes available stimuli, such as an opponent’s movement. Stimulus identification concerns detecting and interpreting relevant information. Response selection concerns deciding what action to take. Response programming and execution prepare and produce the movement.||Feedback from the action and its outcome informs later responses. The model simplifies a continuous interacting process; real sport does not always pause neatly between boxes. Skilled anticipation uses advance information to prepare responses, while misleading cues can produce incorrect anticipation. Faster movement alone does not explain every reduction in total response time.||In a badminton receive, the player observes racket and shuttle information, decides on a direction and executes movement. Failure can arise from misreading a cue, selecting an unsuitable response or executing it inaccurately. A coach should identify which part seems limiting before changing practice. Repeating the same movement does not necessarily fix poor reading of opponents.||Explain a concrete sequence and give a feedback loop. Use relevant input rather than listing every sensory stimulus in the venue. Distinguish reaction time from movement time: the first concerns delay before initiation, the second execution after initiation. Total response can improve through either, and accuracy must be considered alongside speed.''',
'A receiver moves quickly to the wrong side after misreading a deceptive shot. Locate the likely difficulty in the processing model.',
'The player may have misidentified relevant stimulus information or selected the wrong response.||Fast movement execution does not correct the direction decision.||Practice should include reading appropriate shot cues and checking decisions, not only sprint speed.',
'Trace a soccer goalkeeper’s response to a shot through input, selection, output and feedback. Distinguish reaction and movement time.',
'Input includes ball and shooter information; selection chooses an appropriate response; output executes the movement.||Feedback includes the save outcome and sensed or observed action, informing future attempts.||Reaction time precedes response initiation; movement time covers the executed action. Analyse both accuracy and speed.',
'The boxes are a teaching model of a continuous process.','processing')
L(2,'feedback','Intrinsic, extrinsic, concurrent and terminal feedback','Motor learning and coaching',
'''Intrinsic feedback is information available from the performer’s own sensory systems, such as the feel of contact or seeing where a ball lands. Extrinsic feedback is additional information supplied from outside, such as a coach’s comment or a video review. Intrinsic does not mean always accurate; a learner can misinterpret a sensation.||Concurrent feedback is given during the action; terminal feedback follows it. Knowledge of results reports the outcome, such as whether a serve landed in the target. Knowledge of performance concerns the movement process, such as the contact position. A message can combine both. Timing and content are separate dimensions and should be classified separately.||Verbal and non-verbal feedback can reinforce useful behaviour and support motivation. Feedback should be specific enough to guide a change and manageable enough to use. Giving corrections after every attempt can create dependence in some contexts; withholding all guidance can also leave a learner repeating an error. Suitability depends on the stage, task and purpose.||Link observation to an actionable focus: your contact was behind the intended position identifies more than bad serve. Check whether the cue produces a useful change and allow the learner to interpret intrinsic information. A positive tone helps communication, but praise without evidence does not explain how performance can improve.''',
'After a serve a coach says it landed long because your contact was behind your head. Classify source, timing and content.',
'The coach supplies extrinsic feedback.||It is terminal because it follows the serve.||Landed long is knowledge of results; contact behind the head is knowledge of performance. The statement combines both.',
'A swimmer senses poor balance during a movement while a coach gives a signal during the action. Classify both and explain one feedback planning consideration.',
'Sensed balance is intrinsic feedback. The coach’s added signal is extrinsic and concurrent.||A signal should be interpretable without distracting from the action.||Feedback quantity and timing should suit the learner and task, with opportunities to use intrinsic information.',
'Feedback classifications overlap; source, timing and content answer different questions.','feedback')
L(2,'motion','Linear, angular and general motion','Biomechanics',
'''Linear motion describes displacement along a straight or curved path where the points of a rigid body share the same translational movement. Angular motion is rotation about an axis. General motion combines translation and rotation, as occurs in many sporting actions. A ball can translate across a court while also spinning.||Speed is distance travelled per unit time and has magnitude only. Velocity is displacement per unit time and includes direction. A player can run a loop and finish at the starting point: average speed is positive but average velocity over the whole loop is zero. Instantaneous velocity during the loop is not zero.||Acceleration is change in velocity per unit time, so changing direction at constant speed is still acceleration. Deceleration is commonly used for a reduction in speed; the sign of acceleration depends on the chosen positive direction. State units and reference direction instead of treating every negative number as moving backwards.||Separate the body or segment being analysed, the interval and the quantity requested. In a sprint, a runner’s centre of mass translates while limbs rotate around joints. A summary such as running is linear misses the angular actions contributing to the whole movement. Use a drawing and explicit direction when describing vectors.''',
'A player runs 40 m around a route in 8 s and returns to the starting point. Calculate average speed and average velocity.',
'Average speed = 40 ÷ 8 = 5 m/s.||Net displacement is zero, so average velocity = 0 ÷ 8 = 0 m/s.||This does not mean the player was stationary during the route.',
'A ball travels towards goal while spinning, and a runner changes direction without changing speed. Classify motion and explain acceleration.',
'The ball has general motion: translation plus angular rotation.||The runner’s velocity changes because direction changes.||Therefore acceleration occurs even at constant speed; velocity includes direction.',
'Choose the body, interval and reference direction before calculating.','vectors')
L(2,'kinematics','Speed, velocity and acceleration calculations','Biomechanics',
'''Average speed equals total distance divided by elapsed time. Average velocity uses displacement instead of total distance. Average acceleration equals final velocity minus initial velocity, divided by time. These formulas describe changes but do not alone identify which force caused them. Units are metres per second for velocity and metres per second squared for acceleration.||Take a chosen positive direction seriously. A ball moving east at 8 m/s and slowing to 2 m/s east has negative acceleration if east is positive. It remains moving east. A ball moving east and then west can have a large velocity change even if its speed at the two endpoints is equal.||A graph’s axes determine its interpretation. Slope on a displacement–time graph represents velocity; slope on a velocity–time graph represents acceleration. Area under velocity–time gives displacement for the interval, including sign. These relationships require the actual axis quantities, not simply seeing a line rise or fall.||Show substitution, result, units and a contextual interpretation. Distinguish an average over an interval from an instantaneous value. Classroom calculations often assume constant acceleration for simplicity; state that assumption when using a straight-line model. A measured sport motion may have changing acceleration and measurement uncertainty.''',
'A runner’s velocity increases from 2 to 8 m/s east over 3 s. Calculate average acceleration with east positive.',
'a = (8 − 2) ÷ 3.||a = 2 m/s² east.||This is the average change in velocity per second over the stated interval, not proof that acceleration was constant throughout.',
'A ball slows from 10 to 4 m/s east in 2 s. Calculate acceleration and explain why negative acceleration does not mean westward motion.',
'a = (4 − 10) ÷ 2 = −3 m/s² with east positive.||Acceleration is directed west while velocity remains east at the stated endpoints.||The velocity magnitude decreases; direction of motion and direction of acceleration differ.',
'Fictional data illustrate average quantities; measurements have uncertainty.','vectors')
L(2,'projectiles','Projection speed, angle and height','Biomechanics',
'''A projectile moves after release under gravity and any aerodynamic forces. In an ideal model with no air resistance, horizontal velocity is constant and vertical acceleration is downward. The trajectory is parabolic. Real balls can experience drag and spin-induced forces, so the ideal model is a starting point rather than a perfect match to every sport.||Release speed, release angle and release height influence flight. For equal release and landing heights, fixed release speed and no air resistance, maximum horizontal range occurs at 45 degrees. That result does not apply unchanged when release height differs, speed changes with technique or the aim is clearing a net or reaching a target rather than maximum range.||Horizontal and vertical components of release velocity explain the angle trade-off. A greater angle provides more upward velocity but less horizontal velocity at fixed speed. Higher release above landing can increase flight time. An optimal sport angle must account for constraints and the performer’s ability to generate speed at that angle.||When evaluating a throw, state the target and assumptions before recommending a change. A high arc may help clear an obstacle while reducing useful horizontal speed. A faster release can improve range under suitable conditions, but a skill must also preserve accuracy and appropriate technique. Do not infer a universal best angle from an ideal classroom diagram.''',
'Why is 45 degrees an ideal maximum-range result but not a universal best basketball shooting angle?',
'The 45-degree result assumes equal release and landing height, fixed speed and no air resistance.||A basketball shot has a different target height, required entry geometry and accuracy constraints.||Release speed and technique can also vary with angle; optimise for the actual target rather than maximum ground range.',
'Compare two ideal launches at equal speed and height, one 30 degrees and one 60 degrees. Explain a limitation of transferring the result to a spinning tennis ball.',
'In the equal-height no-drag model the complementary angles have equal range; 60 degrees has greater height and flight time.||The 30-degree launch has greater horizontal velocity.||A spinning tennis ball experiences aerodynamic effects and target constraints; ideal symmetry need not hold.',
'Ideal flight neglects drag and spin and is not a technique prescription.','projectile')
L(2,'balance','Base of support and centre of gravity','Biomechanics',
'''The base of support is the area enclosed by contact points with the support surface. The centre of gravity is the point through which the resultant weight acts in an approximately uniform gravitational field. The line of gravity is the vertical line through it. In a simple static situation, maintaining that line within the base helps resist tipping.||A wider base and lower centre of gravity generally increase static stability in suitable directions. Body mass can affect resistance to an external disturbance, but stability also depends on posture, force direction, friction and active control. More mass is not a guarantee of better balance in every skill. Direction matters: a wide stance may be stable sideways but less stable forwards.||Dynamic movement often intentionally moves beyond a static balance condition. A sprint start trades static stability for the ability to accelerate. A defender may lower the centre of gravity and widen support to respond to contact, while retaining enough mobility to change direction. Stability and mobility can create competing demands.||Analyse the base, centre height, line position and external forces for the stated phase. Avoid labelling every lean as an error. Use an annotated diagram to explain what would cause tipping in a static model and what controlled movement might achieve in a dynamic task. Actual balance also relies on sensory and muscular control.''',
'A defender widens the stance and bends the knees before receiving contact. Explain two stability effects and a possible trade-off.',
'A wider base increases the support area in relevant directions.||Lowering the centre of gravity can reduce tipping tendency under an external force.||A very wide or rigid stance may limit quick movement; stability must be balanced with mobility.',
'Why can a sprinter leaning forward be effective even though the line of gravity is near or beyond a static support boundary?',
'The start is dynamic and aims to accelerate rather than remain motionless.||A static tipping model alone does not describe controlled forward motion supported by subsequent steps and forces.||Judge the phase, force direction and movement purpose; a lean is not automatically poor balance.',
'The diagram is a static model; dynamic control requires additional analysis.','balance')
L(2,'newton','Newton’s laws in sporting actions','Biomechanics',
'''Newton’s first law states that a body maintains rest or constant velocity unless acted on by a net external force. Inertia is resistance to changes in motion and is related to mass. A moving ball does not need a continuing forward force to keep moving in an ideal frictionless model; drag and other forces explain slowing in real conditions.||Newton’s second law relates net force to mass and acceleration: F = ma for constant mass. Net force is the vector sum of forces, not any one applied force. If horizontal applied force and resistance balance, horizontal acceleration is zero even though both forces are non-zero. Weight and normal force may also balance vertically.||Newton’s third law describes equal and opposite forces on different interacting bodies. A runner pushes the ground backwards, and the ground exerts a forward force on the runner. These two forces do not cancel on the runner because they act on different bodies. Forces acting on one body must be combined separately.||For a sport explanation, identify the body analysed, the interacting body and the direction. Distinguish force from velocity and do not assume that faster motion always means greater current net force. Acceleration, rather than velocity alone, determines net force in the simple second-law relationship.''',
'A 60 kg fictional athlete has a horizontal net force of 180 N. Calculate acceleration and explain the ground interaction.',
'a = F ÷ m = 180 ÷ 60 = 3 m/s² in the net-force direction.||The runner exerts a backward force on the ground; the ground exerts a forward force on the runner.||The action–reaction pair acts on different bodies and does not cancel on the runner.',
'A ball moves at constant horizontal velocity in an ideal model. Is a continuing net forward force required? Explain what changes in a real flight.',
'No: the first law permits constant velocity with zero net horizontal force.||In real flight drag usually opposes relative motion and spin can add lift or lateral forces.||These forces change velocity; identify forces acting on the ball rather than inventing a force because it is moving.',
'The second-law calculation assumes constant mass and the stated net force.','newton')
L(2,'levers','First, second and third class levers','Biomechanics',
'''A lever has an axis or fulcrum, an effort force and a resistance or load. Classification depends on their relative positions. In a first-class lever the fulcrum lies between effort and resistance. In a second-class lever the resistance lies between fulcrum and effort. In a third-class lever the effort lies between fulcrum and resistance.||Many limb actions act as third-class levers. In a simplified elbow-flexion model, the elbow is the fulcrum, biceps applies effort near the elbow, and a hand-held load is further away. This arrangement often gives speed and range at the distal end while requiring comparatively large muscle force. It is not a mechanical force advantage simply because the movement is effective.||Mechanical advantage in an ideal lever equals effort-arm length divided by resistance-arm length. The relevant moment arms are perpendicular distances from the axis to each force’s line of action. Anatomy and joint angle can change those distances even when attachment points are unchanged. A straight ruler model therefore simplifies a real joint.||Draw the positions before assigning a class. Then explain the advantage or trade-off in the task. The toe-standing example is commonly represented as a second-class lever with the forefoot as fulcrum, body weight between it and Achilles effort. Real body systems involve multiple forces, joints and changing geometry, so state the model’s boundaries.''',
'An elbow lever has effort 0.04 m from the axis and a hand load 0.32 m away. Identify class and ideal mechanical advantage.',
'The effort lies between fulcrum and load, so the simplified arrangement is third class.||MA = 0.04 ÷ 0.32 = 0.125.||The short effort arm implies a force disadvantage, while the distal end can gain movement speed and range.',
'Draw the three orders F–E–R, E–F–R and F–R–E and classify each. Explain why perpendicular moment arms matter.',
'F–E–R is third class; E–F–R is first class; F–R–E is second class.||Torque depends on perpendicular distance to a force’s line of action.||A distance measured along the bone is not always the effective moment arm, especially when the force is angled.',
'The lever diagram omits other forces and changing anatomy.','lever')
L(2,'motivation','Motivation and self-confidence','Sport psychology',
'''Motivation influences direction, intensity and persistence of effort. Intrinsic motivation relates to interest or satisfaction in the activity itself; extrinsic motivation relates to external rewards or consequences. They can coexist. An athlete may enjoy improving a skill while also seeking team selection. It is inaccurate to assume every external reward destroys enjoyment.||Self-confidence concerns belief in the ability to meet a demand. It should be distinguished from certainty of winning or inflated claims of superiority. Evidence of preparation, useful feedback and achievable progress can support confidence. Overconfidence may reduce attention to real weaknesses, while very low confidence can impair commitment to an appropriate action.||Motivational needs differ across individuals and situations. A beginner may need a manageable task and recognition of progress, whereas an experienced player may respond to a demanding specific challenge. Punishment or public comparison can have unintended effects. A strategy should support useful effort, learning and a workable relationship rather than only produce short-term compliance.||Evaluate a strategy through its mechanism and observed effect. Ask whether it strengthens interest, competence, commitment or a clear purpose. Do not infer private motivation solely from a single visible result. A missed shot can occur in a highly motivated athlete, and a successful shot is not proof of confidence or emotional state.''',
'A player enjoys refining a serve and also wants team selection. Identify motivational sources and one confidence-building strategy.',
'Enjoyment of refinement is intrinsic; selection is an extrinsic incentive. Both can coexist.||A specific achievable process goal with feedback on progress can support confidence through evidence of preparation.||Confidence is belief in meeting the demand, not a guarantee of winning.',
'Evaluate a coach’s claim that only the winning player was motivated. Suggest a more informative observation.',
'Outcome reflects opponents, execution and chance as well as motivation.||Observe persistence, engagement with practice and response to feedback over time, while allowing the athlete to explain their goals.||Use a strategy matched to the learner rather than treating winning as a direct measure of internal motivation.',
'Visible performance does not reveal a person’s private mental state.','goals')
L(2,'attention','Concentration and attentional styles','Sport psychology',
'''Concentration involves directing and maintaining attention on relevant information. Nideffer’s model describes broad or narrow attention and internal or external focus. Broad external attention scans the environment; narrow external attention focuses on a particular external cue or target. Broad internal attention can analyse several ideas; narrow internal attention can focus on one internal sensation or thought.||A midfielder scanning teammate and opponent positions uses a broad external focus. A player attending to a target before a free throw may use narrow external focus. Reviewing several tactical options can be broad internal, while focusing on one controlled breathing cue can be narrow internal. These categories describe attentional demands, not permanent personality labels.||Successful performance may require switching styles. A player scans the court, selects an option and then focuses on the relevant execution cue. Remaining narrow can miss a new passing lane; remaining broad during a precision action can distract from a critical target. The preferred focus depends on the task and its stage.||Explain where attention is directed and how much information it includes. A useful strategy can be a cue word or routine that brings attention back to a relevant feature. Avoid instructing everyone to block out all external information: in an open game, important changes must still be detected. The aim is relevant attention, not simply less attention.''',
'A midfielder scans the whole field, then focuses on a teammate’s movement before passing. Name the two external styles.',
'Scanning several positions is broad external attention.||Focusing on the selected teammate’s relevant movement is narrow external attention.||Switching can support both option selection and execution; neither style is universally best.',
'Classify analysing several tactical choices and attending to one body sensation. Explain a risk of remaining narrow throughout an open game.',
'Several tactical thoughts: broad internal. One body sensation: narrow internal.||Persistent narrow focus can miss changing opponents or new options.||The strategy should permit switching to the information required by the phase of play.',
'The categories are task-dependent and do not diagnose attention disorders.','attention')
L(2,'arousal','Stress, arousal and the inverted-U model','Sport psychology',
'''Arousal describes a level of activation; stress concerns a perceived imbalance between demands and resources. Anxiety can include worry and bodily symptoms. These are related but not identical. A high level of activation may help one task and interfere with another. The same competitive situation can be interpreted differently by different athletes.||The inverted-U hypothesis suggests that performance can improve with rising arousal up to an optimum and then decline. It is a model, not a universal law or a precise prediction from a single heart-rate reading. Task complexity, precision, experience and individual differences can change the useful arousal range and the actual relationship.||A skilled athlete performing a well-practised powerful action may tolerate different activation from a novice learning a complex precision skill. Age and experience can influence demands but do not assign a fixed optimum. Interventions should match the observed problem: energising cues and calming routines serve different purposes.||Interpret a fictional graph by identifying its axes and trend, then qualify transfer to real people. A decline under pressure might relate to attention, tension or decision demands, but the graph alone does not prove one cause. Use athlete feedback and repeated observations to evaluate a strategy rather than claiming it will automatically eliminate stress.''',
'A novice precision performer becomes rushed under pressure. Explain an inverted-U interpretation and one limitation.',
'The model suggests arousal may have moved beyond a useful level for the task.||High activation could interfere with attention or control, so a familiar calming routine may be considered.||The model does not prove cause or an exact optimum; task demands and individual responses vary.',
'Why might a powerful familiar action and a complex new skill require different arousal strategies?',
'The new skill requires substantial conscious attention and coordination, which excessive activation can disrupt.||A familiar powerful task may benefit from greater activation in some athletes.||Match strategy to evidence and task; there is no universal arousal score or identical optimum for all ages.',
'The curve is a hypothesis illustration, not a diagnostic scale.','arousal')
L(2,'goals','SMARTER goals and goal types','Sport psychology',
'''An outcome goal concerns a result relative to others, such as winning a match. A performance goal concerns an individual standard, such as improving a measured passing-accuracy result. A process goal concerns actions used to perform, such as scanning before receiving. The types can support one another, but they offer different levels of control.||SMARTER is a planning acronym with variations across resources. Here it means specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, time-bound, evaluated and reviewed. Define the version used rather than assuming every source expands each letter identically. A useful goal identifies a clear behaviour or standard and a way to assess progress.||Winning is influenced by opponents and conditions. A process goal can focus effort on a controllable action, while a performance measure shows whether a standard improved. Evaluation compares evidence with the target. Review decides whether to retain, adjust or replace the goal. Setting a goal once does not make it effective indefinitely.||Write a goal that fits the athlete’s current task and realistic context. Include the measurement method and timeframe in a fictional plan, then state how feedback would prompt adjustment. Avoid promising a guaranteed result or making a health target from arbitrary body-size values. Effective goals organise learning; they do not replace practice or coaching.''',
'Change win every game into one process goal and one performance goal for passing, with evaluation and review.',
'Process: scan for a free option before receiving during the agreed practice.||Performance: improve the proportion of appropriate completed passes in comparable teacher-observed tasks by the agreed review date, using a baseline.||Evaluate the observations and review the goal with the teacher; exact targets depend on baseline and context, and winning is not fully controllable.',
'Classify winning a tournament, improving an individual serve-target score, and using a routine before each serve. Explain the SMARTER version used here.',
'Winning: outcome; individual score: performance; routine: process.||Specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, time-bound, evaluated and reviewed.||Evaluation checks evidence and review adjusts the plan; acronym variants should be stated explicitly.',
'Fictional targets require baseline evidence before practical use.','goals')
L(2,'unit2-apply','Apply coaching and biomechanics to a skill','Integrated application',
'''A strong skill analysis joins observation, mechanical explanation and a suitable coaching response. Identify the exact phase and task first. A vague statement such as the serve is wrong does not locate an issue. The learner’s stage, environmental demands and feedback needs determine how a proposed change should be introduced.||For a throwing action, describe relevant joint actions, segment motion and projection conditions. For balance, identify support and centre-of-gravity position. Use only the information actually available. A single image cannot establish timing through an entire action, and a successful outcome can occur despite a technique limitation.||Connect a proposed cue to the diagnosed feature. A novice may need a clear demonstration and one priority, while a more experienced athlete may use a precise video comparison. Select knowledge of performance when the movement itself needs adjustment and knowledge of results when the target outcome helps assess it.||An integrated answer explains a causal chain, gives a relevant example and recognises limits. Repeated observation under similar conditions can test whether a change is associated with improvement. Confidence and goal strategies should support the skill task, not substitute for mechanical evidence. Practical implementation belongs within the teacher’s supervised programme.''',
'A novice’s pass misses the target and video shows inconsistent release direction. Build an explanation and coaching response without inferring an injury.',
'The observed issue concerns release direction and consistency, not a diagnosed medical problem.||Use a demonstration and a focused direction cue suited to cognitive learning, with terminal knowledge of performance.||Check repeated comparable attempts and target outcomes; confidence can be supported through a manageable process goal.',
'An athlete completes a movement quickly but repeatedly chooses a marked receiver. Explain why biomechanical speed work alone may not solve the problem.',
'The limiting feature may be stimulus identification or response selection in an open environment.||Use practice including scanning, changing defenders and decision feedback.||Preserve execution practice but evaluate tactical choices as well as completion time; speed alone does not ensure an appropriate decision.',
'Written analysis supports, but cannot verify, actual skill proficiency.','feedback')
L(3,'muscle-structure','From muscle belly to myofibril','Functional anatomy',
'''A skeletal muscle belly contains bundles called fascicles. A fascicle contains muscle fibres, which are long muscle cells. Each fibre contains myofibrils built from repeating sarcomeres. Myofilaments within these structures include actin and myosin. This hierarchy links the visible muscle to the microscopic structures involved in tension generation.||Epimysium surrounds the whole muscle, perimysium surrounds fascicles and endomysium surrounds individual fibres. These connective tissues support organisation and transmission of force towards attachments. A muscle fibre is a cell, not simply another name for a bundle of cells. Myofibrils are within fibres, not the outer connective-tissue covering.||A sarcomere extends between adjacent Z lines. Actin and myosin are organised into a pattern of bands and zones. Many sarcomeres shorten together as filaments slide; they do not need to become shorter filaments themselves. The organisation helps explain how a microscopic process contributes to an overall muscle action.||Use a nested diagram and trace one level inward at a time. Then reverse the explanation from filament interaction to tension transmitted through tissue and tendon. The drawing is not to scale and leaves out many cellular components. It supports terminology and relationships without pretending to show the full complexity of living muscle.''',
'Order whole muscle, fascicle, muscle fibre, myofibril and sarcomere from largest organisational level to smallest, with connective coverings.',
'Whole muscle → fascicle → muscle fibre → myofibril → sarcomere.||Epimysium surrounds muscle, perimysium surrounds fascicles and endomysium surrounds fibres.||A fibre is a muscle cell; a myofibril is a contractile structure within it.',
'Correct: perimysium covers every myofibril and muscle fibres are bundles of fascicles. Explain how the hierarchy relates to movement.',
'Perimysium surrounds fascicles, not individual myofibrils.||Fascicles contain fibres, which contain myofibrils.||Sarcomere interactions contribute tension that is transmitted through the muscle’s connective structures and tendons to attachments.',
'Nested boxes show organisation, not anatomical scale.','hierarchy')
L(3,'sarcomere','Sliding filaments and sarcomere bands','Functional anatomy',
'''The sarcomere is the functional repeating unit between Z lines. Thin actin filaments extend from the Z-line region and overlap thick myosin filaments. The A band corresponds to the length of the thick filaments, the I band to thin-filament-only regions and the H zone to a thick-filament-only central region when present.||During shortening, actin slides further between myosin filaments and the Z lines move closer together. The I band and H zone become narrower, while the A band remains approximately constant. The filaments themselves do not become shorter. Changing overlap, repeated across many sarcomeres, helps explain shortening of the contractile structures.||Myosin heads interact with exposed actin binding sites in cross-bridge cycling. Calcium regulation and ATP availability permit the cycle, rather than calcium being an alternative energy currency. A muscle can develop tension without visible overall shortening if the load and internal behaviour produce an isometric condition. Sarcomere diagrams usually illustrate one simplified shortening state.||Compare resting and shortened diagrams by tracking the same labelled structures. Identify what changed and what stayed constant. Avoid saying every band shrinks or that actin dissolves. Then connect the microscopic changes to a sporting example, while recognising that overall force depends on recruitment, length, velocity and external loading as well as overlap.''',
'During sarcomere shortening, predict changes in Z-line distance, I band, H zone, A band and filament lengths.',
'Z lines move closer; I band and H zone narrow.||A band stays approximately constant because thick-filament length stays constant.||Actin and myosin lengths do not shorten; their overlap increases as filaments slide.',
'A student says myosin shrinks and all bands disappear during a jump. Correct the mechanism and state one additional factor influencing muscle force.',
'Filaments slide relative to one another; thick and thin filament lengths are approximately unchanged.||A band remains constant; I band and H zone narrow rather than every band simply disappearing.||Force also depends on recruitment, muscle length, contraction velocity and loading conditions.',
'Band drawings are conceptual and not microscopic measurements.','sarcomere')
L(3,'cross-bridge','Calcium and ATP in cross-bridge cycling','Functional anatomy',
'''A muscle action potential triggers calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Calcium binds to troponin, leading to a change in tropomyosin position that exposes actin binding sites. This allows myosin heads to form cross-bridges. Calcium is a regulatory signal; it is not the molecule that supplies the energy normally attributed to ATP.||ATP binding permits myosin to detach from actin. ATP hydrolysis energises and positions the head for a further cycle. With binding sites exposed, a head can bind; phosphate release is associated with the force-generating power stroke, followed by ADP release. The sequence is repeated while conditions allow, rather than one head pulling once for an entire contraction.||Relaxation involves reducing available calcium through active reuptake into the sarcoplasmic reticulum. ATP also supports this pumping. When calcium no longer exposes binding sites, cross-bridge formation reduces. Saying ATP simply starts contraction misses its roles in detachment and calcium handling as well as energising cycling.||Explain the sequence with separate regulatory and energy roles. The school syllabus’s shorthand linking calcium and ATP should not be read as calcium releasing ATP from nowhere. Connect neural activation, calcium regulation, cross-bridge cycling and tension in an ordered account. The diagram simplifies molecular processes and does not show every intermediate biochemical state.''',
'Explain why calcium and ATP have different roles in contraction, including detachment and relaxation.',
'Calcium binds troponin, shifting tropomyosin so actin binding sites are available.||ATP binding permits detachment; hydrolysis energises the myosin head for further cycling.||ATP also supports calcium reuptake during relaxation; calcium is regulation, not the direct ATP energy source.',
'Correct: myosin cannot detach until calcium turns into ATP. Outline a more accurate sequence.',
'Calcium and ATP are different substances and calcium does not turn into ATP.||Calcium exposes sites via troponin and tropomyosin; ATP binding detaches myosin and hydrolysis prepares further cycling.||Binding and the force-generating stroke recur while sites are exposed; calcium reuptake helps end activation.',
'The cycle is a simplified molecular explanation, not a clinical assessment.','crossbridge')
L(3,'force-relations','Force–length and concentric force–velocity','Functional anatomy',
'''In a concentric force–velocity relationship, maximal force capacity generally decreases as shortening velocity increases. A rapidly shortening muscle does not generate the same maximum force as it can at a slower shortening speed under comparable conditions. This does not mean every fast movement has low total sporting performance; power depends on both force and velocity.||Active force–length behaviour depends partly on filament overlap. At very short or very long lengths, effective cross-bridge conditions reduce active force capacity. An intermediate optimal region supports greater active force. Passive tension can increase as tissue stretches, so total force and active contractile force are not identical curves.||These relationships refer to muscle conditions, not a direct universal prediction from a single joint angle. Muscle architecture, tendon behaviour, activation and changing moment arms affect joint torque. A longer visible limb position is not proof that the muscle is at its ideal sarcomere length.||Apply the concepts by distinguishing force, speed and power, and by stating which curve is considered. A fictional graph can illustrate trends without providing an athlete’s real maximum. Avoid the claim that longer always means stronger or that the optimum force condition necessarily gives optimum sport technique. A skilled action balances speed, control and force transfer.''',
'Explain why longer muscle length does not always mean greater active force and why faster shortening does not imply greater maximal force.',
'Active force depends on useful actin–myosin overlap and has an intermediate optimal region.||Excessively short or long conditions can reduce active force capacity.||For concentric shortening, maximal force capacity generally falls as shortening velocity rises; power still depends on force × velocity.',
'A graph shows active force peaking in the middle and passive force rising at longer lengths. Explain why adding the curves changes interpretation.',
'Active force reflects contractile contributions; passive force reflects stretched elastic structures.||Total force can include both, so it need not have the same shape as active force alone.||Joint torque also depends on activation and moment arms; the graph is not a direct technique prescription.',
'Illustrative curves have no athlete-specific force scale.','force-length')
L(3,'neurons','Neural pathways and motor units','Functional anatomy',
'''A neuron includes a cell body with nucleus, dendrites that receive signals and an axon that conducts signals away from the cell body. Sensory pathways convey information towards the central nervous system, while motor pathways carry commands towards effectors. The brain and spinal cord participate in processing and coordination; not every response requires the same conscious pathway.||A motor unit consists of one motor neuron and the skeletal muscle fibres it innervates. When the neuron activates its fibres under normal conditions, they respond as a unit. The all-or-none principle concerns an action potential or recruited unit, not a claim that the entire muscle always produces either zero force or maximum force.||Whole-muscle force can be graded by recruiting more motor units and by changing firing frequency. Smaller, lower-threshold units are generally recruited before larger units as demand rises. Coordination, feedback and spinal processing also influence movement. A motor unit is not one sensory neuron attached to one whole muscle.||Trace a sensory observation to processing and motor output, then link the motor neuron to its fibres. Identify where information is travelling rather than defining sensory and motor by the shape of a drawing. Sport examples should distinguish detecting a ball, deciding a response and generating muscle force, while recognising that these processes interact continuously.''',
'Define a motor unit and explain how whole-muscle force can vary despite an all-or-none principle.',
'A motor unit is one motor neuron and all the muscle fibres it innervates.||Individual activation is all-or-none in the relevant sense, but additional units can be recruited.||Changes in firing frequency also grade force, so whole-muscle force is not restricted to zero or maximum.',
'Trace noticing a ball to a movement response, distinguishing sensory neurons, brain/spinal cord and motor neurons.',
'Sensory pathways convey information towards the central nervous system.||Brain and spinal cord process and coordinate the response; the exact pathways depend on the action.||Motor neurons carry output to their muscle fibres, producing activation and coordinated movement.',
'The diagram shows a simplified pathway, not every neural connection.','neuron')
L(3,'recruitment','Recruitment and impulse frequency','Functional anatomy',
'''Force demand can be met by changing the number and size of active motor units. Preferential recruitment generally follows the size principle: lower-threshold units activate before higher-threshold units as demand rises. Higher-force tasks can involve a greater number of units and units with larger force capacities. This ordering is a useful general principle, not a rule that every complex movement is identical.||Increasing motor-neuron firing frequency can increase force through temporal summation of muscle responses. Recruitment and rate coding are distinct mechanisms and can both contribute. More impulses per second is not the same as recruiting more neurons, although the two can occur together when force demand changes.||The all-or-none principle should not be translated into one fixed muscle force. A muscle contains many units, and their activation patterns can vary. A light controlled movement and an explosive task differ in recruitment demand, timing and coordination. External sport performance also depends on mechanics and technique.||Explain a change in force through one or both mechanisms, with accurate terminology. Avoid claiming that athletes can consciously activate every fibre independently or that soreness proves all units were recruited. A classroom account of recruitment describes normal physiology and is not an instruction to attempt unsupervised maximal force tests.''',
'A muscle produces more force first by adding units and then by increasing their firing rate. Name and explain both mechanisms.',
'Adding active units is recruitment, increasing the active fibre contribution.||Increasing impulses per second is rate coding, supporting temporal summation and greater force.||These allow graded whole-muscle output without contradicting the relevant all-or-none principle.',
'Critique: a maximal effort is stronger only because each nerve impulse is bigger.',
'Action potentials are not normally graded as simply bigger impulses to produce more force.||Force can increase through recruitment of more or larger units and changed firing frequency.||Coordination and mechanical conditions also influence external force, so effort alone does not measure output.',
'The size principle is a general recruitment pattern, not a maximal-testing instruction.','recruitment')
L(3,'fibres','Type I, IIa and fast glycolytic fibres','Functional anatomy',
'''Type I fibres generally have slower shortening characteristics, high oxidative capacity and comparatively good fatigue resistance. Type IIa fibres have faster characteristics and substantial oxidative as well as glycolytic capacity. Fast glycolytic fibres have high-rate force and power characteristics with lower fatigue resistance in sustained work. Real muscles contain mixed populations.||The syllabus uses Type IIb for the fast glycolytic category. In adult human muscle, the comparable fast myosin category is usually described as IIx in modern physiology. Use IIb as the syllabus label while recognising this naming distinction; do not imply that every educational label maps perfectly to current human molecular terminology.||An endurance task benefits from fatigue-resistant oxidative characteristics, while a brief explosive action places high demands on rapid force production. All fibre types may contribute depending on recruitment and demand. A sprinter does not use only fast fibres, and a distance athlete does not contain only slow fibres. Training can influence metabolic characteristics and some fibre-type expression.||Compare contraction speed, metabolic capacity and fatigue resistance rather than assigning an entire person to one fibre box. Fibre characteristics contribute to performance alongside technique, training, motivation and other physiological factors. Appearance or one race result cannot establish an individual’s fibre distribution. The lesson does not recommend testing or attempts to change fibre type through drugs.''',
'Compare Type I and the syllabus’s fast glycolytic Type IIb category for an endurance task and explosive effort.',
'Type I generally has slower characteristics, high oxidative capacity and good fatigue resistance, supporting sustained work.||The fast glycolytic category has rapid high-force/power characteristics and lower sustained fatigue resistance, supporting brief intense demands.||Modern human terminology usually calls the comparable category IIx; actual muscles and tasks involve mixed fibre contributions.',
'Correct: distance runners only use Type I and sprinters have no slow fibres. Include Type IIa in your answer.',
'Human muscles contain mixed fibre types and recruitment depends on demand.||Type IIa has fast characteristics with both oxidative and glycolytic capacity.||Performance cannot be inferred solely from one category; training, mechanics and coordination also matter.',
'IIb is retained as a syllabus label; human physiology commonly uses IIx.','fibres')
L(3,'competition-food','Nutrition before, during and after competition','Exercise physiology',
'''Before competition, nutrition should support energy availability and gastrointestinal comfort. Carbohydrate availability can support glycogen-dependent demands; fat and protein have important roles but may alter meal digestion and comfort depending on amount and timing. An athlete’s established plan, event duration and individual tolerance matter more than one universal meal rule.||During prolonged demanding activity, carbohydrate availability and hydration can help sustain performance, but needs depend on duration, intensity, conditions and access. After activity, carbohydrate can help replenish glycogen and protein supplies amino acids for repair and adaptation. Recovery also includes adequate overall energy, fluid and rest; a supplement is not automatically necessary.||Glycaemic index describes the relative blood-glucose response to a standard amount of available carbohydrate in a food under test conditions. It is not a complete health rating or proof of how a mixed meal will behave for every athlete. Food amount, meal composition and timing affect practical responses, so high or low GI alone cannot dictate every decision.||Use pre-, during- and post-activity roles to explain a fictional case. Separate general principles from an individual diet prescription. When a real athlete needs a personal plan, their school programme and qualified sports-nutrition support can account for age, health, preferences and training demands. Classroom responses should not prescribe restriction or supplement doses.''',
'A player has repeated matches across a day. Explain different carbohydrate and protein roles and one limit of GI-only planning.',
'Carbohydrate supports fuel availability and replenishment of glycogen between demanding efforts.||Protein supplies amino acids for repair and adaptation within adequate overall intake.||GI alone omits food amount, mixed-meal composition, tolerance and timing, so it is not a complete plan.',
'Critique: all athletes must eat the same high-GI meal before every event and a recovery shake replaces sleep.',
'Pre-event needs vary with duration, timing, tolerance and established dietary plans.||GI is one food-response measure, not a universal meal rule.||Recovery depends on energy, nutrients, fluid and rest; a shake cannot replace sleep or automatically correct a dietary need.',
'Education about nutrient roles is not a personalised dietary plan.','nutrition')
L(3,'hydration','Hydration and interpreting fluid balance','Exercise physiology',
'''Water supports circulation, temperature regulation and many cellular functions. Exercise can produce fluid losses through sweating and breathing. The effect depends on workload, environment, clothing and individual sweat patterns. Being thirsty, having a high heart rate or sweating visibly is not by itself a complete assessment of hydration.||A classroom estimate of sweat-related fluid balance can compare pre- and post-activity mass with recorded intake and output, under stated assumptions. Mass changes can also reflect food, clothing moisture and other processes. Approximate one kilogram of water as one litre only for the simplified calculation; do not interpret the model as a prescription to drink the computed amount immediately.||Both insufficient fluid replacement and excessive drinking can be problematic. Hydration should be individualised within appropriate school and professional guidance. Evaporative cooling depends on sweat actually evaporating; high humidity can reduce the effectiveness of evaporation even when sweating is substantial. Fluid balance and heat exposure therefore need to be considered together.||Explain the likely performance mechanism and identify what data would improve interpretation. Keep educational arithmetic distinct from treatment of symptoms. Practical tasks should use fictional values or teacher-approved procedures. The site does not provide a diagnosis, mandatory water quota or advice for managing a medical condition.''',
'Fictional pre-mass is 70.0 kg, post-mass 69.4 kg, intake 0.4 L and measured urine 0.1 L. Estimate fluid loss using 1 kg ≈ 1 L.',
'Mass decrease = 0.6 kg ≈ 0.6 L in the simplified water model.||Estimated sweat/other unrecorded loss = 0.6 + 0.4 − 0.1 = 0.9 L.||Clothing, food and other mass changes can affect the estimate. It is not an individual drinking prescription.',
'Explain why sweating heavily in humid weather does not prove effective cooling and why a fixed large drinking target is not universally suitable.',
'High humidity can reduce evaporation, so sweat may drip without removing the same heat as evaporated sweat.||Fluid needs depend on losses, duration, conditions and individual factors.||Excessive drinking can also be harmful; use the school’s appropriate guidance rather than a universal quota.',
'Fictional arithmetic cannot diagnose or treat hydration status.','heat')
L(3,'heat','Heat transfer and hot, humid performance','Exercise physiology',
'''Radiation transfers thermal energy through electromagnetic emission between surfaces. Convection transfers heat through moving air or fluid. Conduction involves direct contact. Evaporation removes heat when liquid sweat changes to vapour. These mechanisms can transfer heat into or out of a body depending on the environment, except that evaporation of sweat at the skin removes heat from that surface.||In hot conditions, smaller temperature gradients can reduce dry heat loss; very hot air or radiant surroundings can contribute heat gain. Humidity reduces the vapour-pressure gradient supporting evaporation. Air movement can improve convective and evaporative exchange, but the outcome depends on temperature and humidity rather than wind always cooling identically.||Heat exposure can increase skin blood flow and sweating demands, adding cardiovascular strain during exercise. Acclimatisation through appropriately managed repeated exposure can change sweating and circulatory responses. It is not immediate and does not eliminate all heat risk. Schools should adjust activity to current conditions and applicable procedures.||Explain mechanism, consequence and a context-appropriate management consideration such as scheduling, shade, clothing or planned breaks. Do not prescribe deliberate heat exposure or treat symptoms through this lesson. A fictional case can evaluate a written plan; actual heat decisions belong to the teacher and relevant current guidance.''',
'A match is moved from a dry mild morning to a hot humid afternoon. Explain two heat-loss challenges and a planning response.',
'A smaller or reversed temperature gradient can reduce convection/radiation loss or add heat.||High humidity reduces effective sweat evaporation.||Review scheduling, breaks, shade and the school’s heat procedures; acclimatisation is not instant protection.',
'Classify touching a cool surface, moving air, emitted thermal radiation and sweat becoming vapour. Explain why more visible sweat may not mean more cooling.',
'Direct contact: conduction; moving air: convection; emitted energy: radiation; phase change: evaporation.||Sweat must evaporate to provide the relevant heat loss.||Humidity and dripping sweat limit the link between visible sweat quantity and actual cooling.',
'The lesson does not prescribe heat exposure or manage heat illness.','heat')
L(3,'cold-altitude','Cold and altitude: mechanisms and adaptation','Exercise physiology',
'''Cold environments increase the potential for heat loss through radiation, convection and conduction, especially with wind or wet clothing. The body can reduce skin blood flow and generate heat through muscular activity such as shivering. Cold conditions can affect dexterity and comfort, but effects depend on exposure, clothing and activity rather than temperature alone.||At altitude, reduced atmospheric pressure lowers inspired oxygen partial pressure even though the oxygen fraction remains approximately similar. Less oxygen pressure can challenge oxygen loading and aerobic performance. Saying altitude air contains no oxygen confuses proportion with pressure. Initial responses can include increased ventilation and cardiovascular adjustments.||With appropriate time and exposure, acclimatisation can include changes in ventilation and red-cell production, but the response varies and does not immediately restore sea-level performance. Altitude training is not an automatic performance benefit for every athlete. Practical exposure and health decisions need qualified management, not a classroom travel recommendation.||Compare mechanisms: cold mainly changes thermal demands, while altitude primarily changes oxygen-pressure conditions. Clothing and shelter address some cold constraints; altitude plans need realistic expectations and current professional guidance. A written response should explain cause and consequence without providing treatment or an unsupervised acclimatisation schedule.''',
'Correct: altitude reduces the oxygen percentage to zero, so an athlete adapts immediately by growing more red cells.',
'Altitude lowers atmospheric and inspired oxygen partial pressure; oxygen fraction is not zero.||Initial responses can include increased ventilation.||Red-cell-related adaptation requires time and varies; it is not an immediate guarantee of restored performance.',
'Compare a cold windy venue and a high-altitude venue using one mechanism, performance consequence and management consideration for each.',
'Cold wind increases convective heat loss, potentially affecting comfort and dexterity; suitable clothing and shelter planning matter.||Altitude reduces oxygen partial pressure, challenging aerobic supply; realistic timing and qualified exposure planning matter.||Do not equate thermal acclimatisation with oxygen-pressure adaptation or promise universal benefit.',
'No travel, exposure schedule or treatment is prescribed.','environment')
L(3,'supplements','Protein powders, caffeine and creatine claims','Exercise physiology',
'''A supplement claim should be evaluated through purpose, evidence, safety and permitted status. Protein powders can offer a convenient protein source when there is an identified need, but they are not inherently superior to an adequate food-based intake. Extra protein beyond need does not automatically produce extra strength without an appropriate overall programme.||Caffeine can influence alertness and perceived effort and has evidence of performance effects in some contexts, with variable responses and possible sleep, anxiety or gastrointestinal effects. Creatine supports phosphocreatine-related availability and can benefit some repeated high-intensity demands. Neither is a universal endurance or skill solution, and individual effects differ.||AIS guidance emphasises assessing evidence and risk rather than trusting marketing. Products can contain undeclared or prohibited ingredients; a legal ingredient does not establish the safety or purity of every product. Caffeine, creatine and protein are not themselves examples of the prohibited anabolic or EPO categories in the current list, but products and rules require checking.||This lesson compares mechanisms and claims without giving doses or recommending student supplement use. In an answer, link a plausible effect to the specific demand, acknowledge limits and identify product risk. Use current AIS and Sport Integrity Australia resources for status and policy, with qualified support for real decisions. More expensive is not evidence of more effective.''',
'A seller says a protein powder guarantees strength, caffeine guarantees calm sleep and creatine improves every sporting skill. Evaluate each claim.',
'Protein can support adequate amino-acid availability but does not guarantee strength or replace training and overall nutrition.||Caffeine effects vary and can disrupt sleep or increase unwanted activation.||Creatine can support some repeated high-intensity demands, not every skill or universal performance outcome; product risk still needs evaluation.',
'Why does a permitted ingredient not prove a supplement product is safe or permitted? Use one relevant performance mechanism.',
'Products may have undeclared or contaminated ingredients and inaccurate labels.||Creatine’s mechanism concerns phosphocreatine-related availability, which may suit repeated intense work more than a claim of instant technique improvement.||Evaluate evidence, individual need and current rules through authoritative sources; this is not a use recommendation.',
'No supplement dose, product recommendation or student-use instruction is supplied.','enhancers')
L(3,'doping','Anabolic agents, EPO and blood doping','Exercise physiology',
'''Anabolic androgenic steroids can affect tissue-building processes and have been misused for strength or muscle-development aims. Misuse carries serious health risks, including cardiovascular and endocrine effects. A physiological effect does not establish acceptable, safe or fair use. Therapeutic medical decisions are separate from performance-doping claims.||EPO stimulates red-cell production; misuse may seek increased oxygen-carrying capacity for aerobic demands. Blood doping manipulates blood or blood components for a similar performance aim. Increasing red-cell-related capacity can also increase serious risks, including circulatory complications. The body’s natural EPO physiology is not itself a prohibited act.||The current WADA list prohibits anabolic agents, EPO-related substances and specified blood-manipulation methods. Rules change, and exact substances, methods and medical exceptions must be checked through current authoritative resources. Protein powder, caffeine and creatine are not interchangeable with these categories; product contamination remains a separate risk.||An exam response should state the proposed physiological effect, connect it to a sporting demand, identify risks and distinguish mechanism from ethical and regulatory status. This is analysis of prohibited practices, not instructions for obtaining, administering or concealing them. Do not present a performance benefit as a reason for a student to use a drug or procedure.''',
'Compare a claimed anabolic-steroid effect with an EPO-related effect, including sporting demand, risk and current status.',
'Anabolic misuse may seek tissue-building or strength effects; EPO misuse seeks increased red-cell production and oxygen transport for aerobic demands.||Both carry serious health risks; examples include endocrine/cardiovascular effects for steroid misuse and circulatory complications for blood-related misuse.||The relevant anabolic and EPO categories are prohibited in the current WADA list; check current authoritative rules for specifics.',
'Critique: because natural EPO exists, injecting any EPO product is permitted, and blood doping is only a nutrition strategy.',
'A natural hormone’s existence does not determine the permitted status of administered substances.||EPO-related substances and specified blood-manipulation methods are prohibited categories.||Blood doping is not ordinary nutrition; mechanism, medical context and current rules must be distinguished. No administration method is provided here.',
'Current-rule links are provided; no doping or treatment instructions appear.','enhancers')
L(3,'periodisation','Microcycles, mesocycles and macrocycles','Exercise physiology',
'''Periodisation organises training over time to manage changing demands and important performance periods. A macrocycle is the broad planning period, a mesocycle a block within it and a microcycle a shorter recurring planning unit. Their lengths depend on the programme; a microcycle is often a week but the terms are not fixed universal calendar units.||Pre-season generally develops preparation for upcoming competition. In-season balances performance, practice, recovery and maintenance. Off-season or transition permits recovery and a changed training emphasis before the next cycle. A calendar label alone does not show which adaptations are being targeted or whether the workload is appropriate.||Peaking aims to align readiness with a priority performance period. Tapering usually reduces training load while preserving useful stimulus before an event. It is not the same as abandoning all training for a long period. A programme can fail if it ignores competition congestion or repeatedly treats every event as requiring a maximal peak.||Interpret a fictional calendar by identifying nested cycles and changes in volume, intensity and recovery. Justify a change through demand and timing, then explain what monitoring would guide adjustment. Do not copy a generic elite schedule directly to students; the classroom task evaluates planning principles, not prescribes individual loads.''',
'A season plan contains 12 weeks, three 4-week blocks and weekly schedules. Identify macro-, meso- and microcycle in this model.',
'The 12-week season plan is the stated macrocycle.||Each 4-week block is a mesocycle; each weekly schedule is a microcycle.||These are contextual labels, not universal required durations.',
'Explain why reducing volume before a priority event can differ from stopping all activity, and why in-season recovery matters.',
'A taper reduces load while preserving useful performance stimulus and readiness.||Complete prolonged cessation may remove stimulus and differ from a planned taper.||In-season competition adds workload, so recovery and maintenance must be balanced with practice and monitored readiness.',
'Calendar lengths and volume bars are fictional examples.','periodisation')
L(3,'recovery','Recovery, maintenance and overtraining evidence','Exercise physiology',
'''Recovery allows restoration and adaptation after training or competition. Rest, sleep, adequate nutrition and an appropriately planned load are foundational considerations. A fashionable recovery method cannot compensate automatically for excessive training or insufficient rest. Maintenance aims to preserve important adaptations when total training emphasis changes.||Overtraining concerns a prolonged maladaptive state involving performance and recovery problems; it is not diagnosed by one tiring session. Possible warning patterns include persistent performance decline, unusual fatigue, disturbed mood or sleep and poor recovery. These signs are non-specific and can have other causes, so students should not self-diagnose from a checklist.||A coach can use a diary, comparable performance measures and discussion to detect patterns needing review. Sudden unexplained changes warrant appropriate school and qualified support. A fictional dataset can show declining performance despite higher load, but it does not prove a medical explanation. Association and diagnosis are different claims.||Evaluate a plan by linking load, competition and recovery across time. Explain why reducing or adjusting load might be considered within supervision, identify what information is missing and avoid guaranteeing a remedy. Maintaining fitness during a busy season can involve focused stimulus rather than adding every possible activity. Practical decisions should follow the school programme.''',
'A fictional player records worsening comparable performance, poor sleep and rising workload over several weeks. What can and cannot be concluded?',
'The pattern suggests a need to review load and recovery.||It is consistent with possible maladaptation but does not diagnose overtraining; other causes may exist.||Use teacher and qualified support, more context and monitoring rather than treating one checklist as a medical conclusion.',
'Explain maintenance during a busy competition period and why one hard session is not proof of overtraining.',
'Maintenance seeks to preserve key adaptations with an appropriate stimulus while balancing competition and recovery.||Acute tiredness after one session differs from a sustained maladaptive pattern.||Interpret duration, comparable performance and context; the task does not diagnose or prescribe treatment.',
'Warning signs are non-specific and do not form a diagnostic tool.','periodisation')
L(3,'advanced-tactics','Adapt tactics to opposition and conditions','Skills and tactics',
'''Advanced tactical decisions combine skill selection with opponent information, environmental constraints and phase of play. An opponent’s strengths and weaknesses can influence risk, spacing and target selection. The best response is not simply repeating a favourite action; it depends on whether that action remains effective in the current situation.||In tennis, repeatedly attacking a weaker side may be useful until the opponent adjusts position. In an invasion game, a change in wind, surface or opponent pressure can alter passing and support decisions. The stated evidence must support the recommendation. A claim about an opponent’s weakness needs observation rather than assumption from appearance.||Phases of play include possession, defence and transition, with sport-specific distinctions. A turnover can require rapidly shifting from attack to recovery positioning. The same space that helps an attack can expose a defence. Explain how individual choices contribute to the team’s coordinated response rather than analysing a player in isolation.||Propose at least two options, select one with a reason and state a condition that would change it. Then identify a way to evaluate the decision through comparable observation. The site supports written tactical reasoning; competitive skill execution and the school-based practical external assessment need actual teacher-managed participation in the school’s selected sport.''',
'A soccer team repeatedly loses possession through long aerial passes in strong wind. Propose and justify an adaptation and a limitation.',
'Shorter controlled passes with closer support may reduce exposure to unpredictable aerial flight.||The choice responds to wind and observed possession losses.||It can also attract pressure or slow progress; opponent positioning and phase may justify a different option.',
'A tennis opponent initially struggles with wide backhand shots but changes position to cover them. Explain an appropriate next decision and how to test it.',
'Reassess open space rather than continuing the same target automatically.||An alternative placement can exploit the newly opened area if execution and risk are suitable.||Observe comparable rallies and opponent response, separating tactical decision from outcome and technical error.',
'Apply to the prescribed sport selected by the school, with practical feedback.','tactics')
L(4,'transfer','Positive, negative and zero transfer','Motor learning and coaching',
'''Transfer of learning concerns the influence of previous learning on a new or later task. Positive transfer helps the target task; negative transfer interferes; zero transfer has little detectable effect in the stated conditions. Similar-looking skills do not necessarily transfer positively if timing, equipment or rules differ.||Categories include skill-to-skill, theory-to-practice and training-to-competition transfer. These categories identify where learning moves, while positive, negative or zero identifies the observed effect. A theory lesson can transfer positively when it supports a useful practical decision, but simply remembering a definition does not establish improved performance.||Practice identical to a predictable drill may fail to represent competitive information demands. Transfer can improve when practice includes relevant perception, decisions and realistic constraints. However, adding complexity before a learner can perform the core action can make practice unmanageable. Selection should reflect current needs rather than copying competition in every detail at every stage.||Describe the source task, target task and specific feature expected to transfer. Then use evidence to judge its effect. Negative transfer is not simply any poor performance after practice; other causes must be considered. A well-designed comparison includes similar conditions and repeated observations, with a clear criterion for improvement.''',
'A player applies a familiar racket timing to a new sport but contacts too late. Identify category and possible effect, with a qualification.',
'The category is skill-to-skill transfer.||The old timing may interfere, suggesting negative transfer.||Repeated evidence and other factors are needed; one late contact does not prove the old skill caused it.',
'A team’s passing drill is accurate without defenders but decisions fail in games. Analyse training-to-competition transfer and a practice adjustment.',
'The predictable drill may omit perception and response-selection demands.||Add appropriate defender movement and decision constraints through a manageable progression.||Evaluate game-relevant decisions as well as drill accuracy; category and effect are separate labels.',
'Transfer effects require evidence, not similarity alone.','transfer')
L(4,'analysis-model','Prepare, observe, evaluate, intervene, re-observe','Motor learning and coaching',
'''The Knudson and Morrison approach structures qualitative movement analysis through preparation, observation, evaluation, intervention and re-observation. Preparation identifies the task, relevant movement features and how evidence will be collected. Observation records what occurs without immediately replacing it with a judgement.||Evaluation compares the observations with meaningful criteria, identifies priorities and considers possible explanations. Intervention introduces an appropriate cue, practice condition or correction. Re-observation checks whether the change improved the priority feature or produced an unintended effect. The cycle is iterative rather than one final diagnosis from one image.||Good observation uses a suitable viewing angle and enough comparable attempts. A camera may hide a joint or distort apparent angles. A checklist can focus attention, but its criteria must be relevant and observable. Movement analysis should avoid claiming an injury, exact muscle force or private mental state from a video alone.||Choose one justified priority instead of correcting everything at once. Match the intervention to the learner and allow time to use it. Compare before and after under similar conditions and distinguish association from certainty of cause. This is a coaching-analysis model, not a clinical assessment method.''',
'Outline the five stages for analysing inconsistent serve contact, including evidence needed before intervention.',
'Prepare: define contact criteria and a useful camera/viewing position. Observe: record several comparable attempts.||Evaluate: identify the most supported priority. Intervene: use a suitable focused cue or practice condition.||Re-observe: compare the same feature and outcome under similar conditions, checking unintended changes.',
'A coach judges one blurry clip and gives five corrections without another observation. Evaluate the missing parts of the model.',
'Preparation and observation are weak because viewing quality and repeated comparable evidence are lacking.||Evaluation cannot reliably prioritise from that evidence; five simultaneous corrections may overload the learner.||Re-observation is missing, so the effect of intervention has not been tested.',
'Qualitative coaching analysis is not a medical diagnosis.','analysis')
L(4,'practice-progressions','Shaping, chaining and static-to-dynamic progression','Motor learning and coaching',
'''Shaping gradually develops a skill by modifying difficulty or conditions towards the target action. Chaining links component actions into a sequence. A static-to-dynamic progression moves from comparatively stable practice towards movement and changing conditions. These approaches can overlap but emphasise different features of learning.||A learner may first practise a simplified whole serve, then progressively approach full conditions: shaping. Linking footwork, preparation and contact into a coordinated sequence illustrates chaining. Performing a pass stationary, then while moving and then with opposition illustrates static-to-dynamic progression. The labels should be explained through the actual practice design.||Breaking a skill into parts is useful only if the parts can be reconnected meaningfully. Some skills depend strongly on rhythm and segment interaction, so isolated practice can distort the target action. Progressions should preserve relevant information and increase difficulty when the learner is ready, not simply on a fixed calendar.||Justify the chosen method through learner needs and task structure. State a criterion for progressing, such as consistent execution and appropriate decisions under the current conditions. Then explain how the final activity supports transfer. A written progression is a planning exercise; actual drills and loads require the teacher’s practical judgement.''',
'Classify simplifying a whole serve then increasing difficulty, linking three movement parts, and passing stationary then while moving.',
'Simplifying then progressively developing the whole serve is shaping.||Linking parts is chaining.||Moving from stationary to moving passing is static-to-dynamic progression; methods may overlap in one plan.',
'Why can practising every part of a throw separately fail to preserve the whole skill? Propose a criterion for reconnecting parts.',
'The throw depends on rhythm and sequential segment interaction.||Isolating parts can alter timing, so reconnect them into relevant whole actions.||Use an observable consistency and timing criterion, then add manageable game constraints; do not assume more parts always improve learning.',
'Progression examples are planning concepts, not mandatory drills.','learning')
L(4,'leadership','Choose a leadership style for the situation','Motor learning and coaching',
'''Autocratic leadership concentrates decisions with the leader and can provide clear rapid direction. Democratic leadership involves participants in suitable decisions and can build shared understanding and commitment. Laissez-faire leadership provides substantial autonomy and limited direct control. None is automatically best for every athlete or every situation.||A new group facing a complex unfamiliar task may need clear instruction. An experienced team reviewing tactics may benefit from discussion and shared decisions. A capable independent athlete may use autonomy effectively, but unclear expectations or insufficient support can make a laissez-faire approach ineffective. The actual needs and constraints matter.||Leadership affects communication, motivation and cohesion. Democratic involvement does not mean every decision must be voted on, and autocratic instruction does not justify disrespect. A leader can change style across tasks while retaining consistent standards and a supportive relationship. Effective autonomy still needs agreed goals and responsibilities.||Compare advantages and limitations with a concrete scenario. Identify urgency, experience, task complexity and the need for commitment. Explain why the style fits and what would prompt adjustment. Avoid assigning styles from stereotypes about age, gender or sport; use observed readiness and the decision context.''',
'Compare leadership for introducing an unfamiliar activity and reviewing tactics with an experienced team.',
'Clear direct instruction can suit an unfamiliar task where shared basic understanding is needed.||Democratic discussion can draw on experienced players’ observations during tactical review.||Both require respectful communication and clear goals; suitability depends on context, not a universally superior style.',
'Evaluate laissez-faire leadership when a team has no agreed roles and inconsistent attendance. Suggest a context where autonomy may work better.',
'Limited direction can worsen ambiguity when expectations and responsibility are unclear.||Establish agreed roles, communication and goals before relying on autonomy.||A capable self-managing athlete with clear expectations may benefit from greater independence and access to support.',
'Leadership style is a context-dependent choice, not a fixed character label.','cohesion')
L(4,'evidence-tools','Video, checklists and feedback evidence','Motor learning and coaching',
'''Video can preserve movement for repeated observation and comparison. Camera angle, frame rate, perspective and occlusion affect what can be judged. A two-dimensional view cannot automatically show a three-dimensional joint angle accurately. Select evidence appropriate to the question rather than treating any slow-motion clip as a complete analysis.||A checklist makes selected criteria explicit and can improve consistency across observations. Criteria should be observable and task-relevant. Peer, mentor and coach feedback supply different perspectives, but all need evidence and respectful communication. A questionnaire can reveal perceived experience or confidence, not directly measure muscle activation or actual skill quality.||Combine evidence sources when they answer different parts of the question. Video may show an execution feature, a results log an outcome pattern and a questionnaire the learner’s interpretation. Agreement strengthens an account but does not remove all uncertainty. Disagreement can identify a need for better data rather than proof that one participant is dishonest.||Plan comparable before-and-after observations and avoid measuring many irrelevant variables. Report what was observed, how it was collected and the limit. Student-created analysis should respect school consent and recording procedures. The site uses fictional scenarios and does not request uploads or record practical performance.''',
'A side-on clip hides the contact point while a checklist rates contact accuracy. Explain the validity problem and an improvement.',
'The camera does not show the feature needed for the criterion.||Use an appropriate viewing position and several comparable attempts, with permission under school procedures.||A checklist does not fix missing evidence; state uncertainty rather than assigning a confident rating.',
'Compare what a performance video, outcome log and confidence questionnaire can establish.',
'Video can show visible execution features within its viewing limits.||An outcome log records results but does not alone identify causes.||A questionnaire reports perceived confidence, not direct muscle activity or proven skill quality; combine sources for different questions.',
'No student recording or personal data upload is required.','analysis')
L(4,'impulse','Impulse and momentum','Biomechanics',
'''Linear momentum equals mass multiplied by velocity and includes direction. Impulse equals the change in momentum. For a constant or average net force over an interval, impulse equals force multiplied by time. On a force–time graph, signed area gives impulse. State the body and direction before substituting numbers.||A greater impulse can produce a larger velocity change for the same mass. For a fixed change in momentum, increasing stopping time can reduce average net force. Catching with a longer controlled deceleration is an example of this principle, although actual technique and peak forces are more complex than a rectangular force–time model.||Applied force and net force are not always the same. Gravity and other forces may contribute during a contact. A force–time graph can have a varying shape, so average force does not reveal peak force. Two curves with equal area can have equal impulse but different peaks and durations.||Use units of kg·m/s for momentum and N·s for impulse. Explain the result as a change in velocity or momentum, not simply an increase in speed regardless of direction. A ball reversed in direction can experience a large momentum change. The calculation supports conceptual analysis rather than prescribing contact forces for students.''',
'A 0.5 kg ball moves at +8 m/s and is stopped in 0.20 s. Calculate change in momentum and average net force.',
'Δp = 0.5 × (0 − 8) = −4 kg·m/s.||Average net force = −4 ÷ 0.20 = −20 N.||The force acts opposite the initial motion. The value is an average, not the peak contact force.',
'The same momentum change occurs over 0.40 s. Calculate average force and explain what remains unchanged.',
'Average force = −4 ÷ 0.40 = −10 N.||The longer interval halves average force for this fixed impulse.||Momentum change remains −4 kg·m/s; peak force and actual force shape are not specified.',
'Force calculations use fictional net-force conditions and are not practical contact advice.','impulse')
L(4,'restitution','Coefficient of restitution and interacting bodies','Biomechanics',
'''Coefficient of restitution describes the relative speed of separation compared with relative speed of approach along the collision direction. It concerns both interacting bodies, not only a permanent property of one ball. Temperature, material, equipment, surface and impact speed can affect collision behaviour.||In a simplified vertical drop and rebound from a fixed surface, with negligible drag and the same height reference, e can be estimated as the square root of rebound height divided by drop height. The square root matters because speed relates to the square root of height in this energy model. The rebound-to-drop height ratio alone is not e.||An ideal elastic collision has e = 1 in the simple model, while a collision with no separation speed along the impact direction has e = 0. Real sport collisions can be more complicated through spin, deformation and surface motion. A ball that rebounds lower does not show that all its lost mechanical energy vanished; energy can become heat, sound and deformation.||Compare controlled conditions rather than changing ball, temperature and surface simultaneously. Identify which feature was varied and what the evidence supports. A higher rebound can affect sport response and timing, but does not automatically make equipment better or permitted. Practical equipment choices should follow school and sport rules.''',
'An idealised ball drops 1.00 m and rebounds 0.64 m from a fixed surface. Estimate e and explain the height-ratio error.',
'e = √(0.64 ÷ 1.00) = 0.8.||The height ratio is 0.64, not e, because collision speed relates to the square root of height.||The estimate assumes vertical flight, fixed surface, negligible drag and a common reference.',
'Two balls rebound differently on different surfaces at different temperatures. Can the difference be attributed only to ball material?',
'No: ball material, surface and temperature changed together.||Control other conditions and compare repeated impacts if investigating material.||Restitution concerns interacting bodies and impact conditions; rebound height alone does not identify one cause.',
'The square-root model applies only under its stated vertical-bounce assumptions.','bounce')
L(4,'torque','Third-class levers and joint torque','Biomechanics',
'''Torque is the turning effect of a force about an axis. Its magnitude is force multiplied by the perpendicular moment arm. A joint can balance external load torque with muscle torque in a simplified static condition. The force applied near a joint may need to be much larger than a distal load when its moment arm is short.||In a third-class lever, muscle effort acts between the joint axis and resistance. The short effort arm often creates a force disadvantage but permits useful distal speed and range. As joint angle changes, the perpendicular moment arm can change. Distance along the bone is not automatically the moment arm of an angled force.||For static equilibrium, opposing torques balance when all relevant forces and moments are included. A simplified model may omit limb weight and co-contraction; state those omissions. Dynamic movement requires net torque to change rotational motion, so a static calculation cannot be applied unchanged during acceleration.||Show the force lines, perpendicular distances, units and balance equation. Explain why a longer load arm increases required balancing torque. Do not turn a classroom model into an unsupervised lifting recommendation. Real muscle force depends on anatomy, posture and other forces that the simple model leaves out.''',
'A 20 N load acts with a 0.30 m moment arm. Muscle moment arm is 0.03 m. Ignoring other forces, find balancing muscle force.',
'Load torque = 20 × 0.30 = 6 N·m.||Muscle force × 0.03 = 6, so force = 200 N.||The short effort arm creates a force disadvantage. This static model omits limb weight and other forces.',
'The load moment arm becomes 0.15 m while force remains 20 N. Calculate torque and explain the meaning of perpendicular distance.',
'Torque = 20 × 0.15 = 3 N·m.||The moment arm is the shortest perpendicular distance from the axis to the force’s line of action.||An angled force can have a moment arm different from distance along the limb; static and dynamic cases differ.',
'The model omits limb mass, other muscles and changing joint geometry.','lever')
L(4,'angular','Moment of inertia and angular momentum','Biomechanics',
'''Moment of inertia describes resistance to changes in rotational motion and depends on both mass and its distribution relative to the axis. Moving mass closer to the axis can reduce moment of inertia without changing total mass. Angular velocity describes the rate of rotation, commonly in radians per second.||Angular momentum in a simple rigid-axis model is L = Iω. If net external torque is negligible, angular momentum is conserved. Reducing I then increases ω to preserve L. The faster spin does not mean angular momentum increased; the distribution changed. External torque from contact or aerodynamic effects can alter the conservation condition.||A rotating athlete bringing limbs closer to the axis illustrates this relationship conceptually. Opening the shape increases I and can reduce ω under the same conservation assumption. A real sporting action may have complex three-dimensional axes, changing body configuration and external forces. The scalar formula simplifies those conditions.||Explain axis, distribution and conservation assumption before calculation. Distinguish angular momentum from angular velocity and avoid saying compact shape creates energy or momentum from nothing. A classroom model does not teach aerial rotation technique or certify safety. Practical gymnastics or diving instruction needs appropriate facilities and qualified supervision.''',
'Initial I is 4 kg·m² and ω is 3 rad/s. I changes to 2 kg·m² with negligible external torque. Find L and new ω.',
'Initial L = 4 × 3 = 12 kg·m²/s.||Conservation gives 12 = 2ω, so new ω = 6 rad/s.||Angular velocity doubles while angular momentum remains 12; mass distribution changed.',
'A student says tucking creates extra angular momentum. Correct this and state when the conservation model may fail.',
'Tucking can reduce I, increasing ω for unchanged L when net external torque is negligible.||It does not automatically increase angular momentum.||External torque from ground contact or other interactions can change L, so identify the phase and system.',
'Conceptual rotation models are not instructions for aerial skills.','angular')
L(4,'force-summation','Simultaneous and sequential force contributions','Biomechanics',
'''Sporting force production often involves several joints and segments. Simultaneous contributions occur when actions overlap in time, as in parts of a vertical push. Sequential contributions organise movement across segments, often from larger proximal segments towards smaller distal ones, as in a throw. The purpose is effective transfer and timing rather than merely activating every muscle at once.||Segmental interaction means movement of one segment affects others. A well-timed sequence can increase distal speed, while a poorly timed action can disrupt transfer. This is not equivalent to adding all individual forces as scalar numbers regardless of direction or time. Forces and torques act on specified bodies and have directions.||A throw may use legs, trunk, upper arm, forearm and hand in an organised sequence. The observable timing is more informative than listing muscles alone. A jump may show coordinated overlapping extension, but its exact pattern depends on the task. Avoid a rigid universal timetable or assuming that maximum speed at every joint simultaneously is optimal.||Use video or a phase diagram to describe what happens when and how it contributes. A proposed cue should address the observed timing issue and suit the learner. Evaluate the result through comparable attempts and outcome as well as execution. Written explanation supports coaching analysis but does not itself establish practical technique quality.''',
'Why might a throw lose distal speed when the arm acts before the trunk contribution is effectively organised?',
'The timing can disrupt useful segmental interaction and transfer.||Sequential organisation coordinates proximal and distal actions rather than making every segment peak simultaneously.||Observation across phases is needed; the explanation is a plausible mechanism, not proof from one still image.',
'Compare overlapping extension in a jump and a proximal-to-distal throw. Correct: sum all muscle forces at different times to obtain ball force.',
'A jump can involve simultaneous or overlapping contributions; a throw often uses a sequence across segments.||Forces have direction and act on specific bodies and intervals.||Adding unrelated scalar muscle-force values ignores timing, geometry and interaction; analyse the actual phase and system.',
'Phase diagrams illustrate timing, not athlete-specific measured forces.','sequence')
L(4,'optimal-flight','Optimise projection for a real target','Biomechanics',
'''Optimal projection is determined by the goal and constraints. A maximum-distance throw, a basketball shot and a tennis ball clearing a net do not share one universal angle. Release speed, angle and height interact with the target, technique and air forces. The word optimal requires a stated criterion such as range, accuracy or suitable entry path.||In the ideal equal-height no-drag model, range is v² sin(2θ)/g. This explains both the 45-degree result and the equal ranges of complementary angles at fixed speed. When release is above landing, flight time changes and the ideal maximum-range angle can differ. Real release speed may also vary with angle.||A target-based analysis should consider obstacle clearance and arrival height as well as horizontal displacement. A spinning ball can deviate from the ideal parabola. Improving one variable may worsen another: a faster release can reduce control, or a high arc can allow an opponent more time. Evaluate the actual demand.||State assumptions, identify what evidence supports the adjustment and discuss one trade-off. A simulation lets students vary ideal parameters and see the model’s behaviour, but it is not an instruction to copy an exact release angle in practice. Actual sport technique must be assessed with appropriate coaching and realistic conditions.''',
'In the equal-height ideal model, v = 10 m/s, θ = 45°, g = 10 m/s². Calculate range and identify two assumptions.',
'R = 10² × sin(90°) ÷ 10 = 10 m.||Assumptions include equal release/landing height and no air resistance, with fixed release speed.||The result is not a universal target-shooting angle or real-ball prediction.',
'Explain why a higher release point and top spin can invalidate a simple 45-degree recommendation.',
'Different release/landing heights change flight time and the ideal range optimum.||Top spin introduces aerodynamic effects absent from the no-drag model.||The goal may require a target or net clearance rather than maximum range, and release speed may change with technique.',
'Interactive outputs are ideal model results, not sport-technique recommendations.','projectile')
L(4,'fluid-flow','Laminar, turbulent and drag forces','Biomechanics',
'''Laminar flow has relatively orderly layers; turbulent flow has irregular fluctuations and mixing. Flow around a sporting object depends on shape, speed, surface and fluid conditions. A real flow can include both orderly and turbulent regions, so describing the whole situation with one label may hide important detail.||Pressure drag results from pressure differences around the object and associated wake behaviour. Surface drag concerns frictional effects along the object’s surface. Wave drag is relevant to producing waves at a free surface, such as a swimmer near the water surface. The categories identify different mechanisms that can act together.||Streamlining can reduce pressure drag in appropriate conditions, but turbulence is not always simply worse. A golf ball’s surface can influence boundary-layer behaviour and flow separation, reducing pressure drag under suitable conditions despite adding surface disturbance. The total drag depends on the combined effects, not one universal smooth is best rule.||Explain relative motion between object and fluid and identify the mechanism relevant to the sport. Equipment design must also comply with rules. A classroom explanation can compare shapes and likely effects but cannot infer an exact drag coefficient without data. Do not claim that less drag is always the only performance goal when control, lift and stability also matter.''',
'Compare pressure drag, surface drag and wave drag using a ball and swimmer example.',
'Pressure drag concerns pressure distribution and wake around an object such as a moving ball.||Surface drag arises from frictional effects along surfaces, including a swimmer’s body.||Wave drag concerns energy associated with creating surface waves, relevant to swimming near the free surface.',
'Critique: turbulence always increases total drag and a completely smooth golf ball must travel furthest.',
'Turbulence and boundary-layer behaviour can change separation and pressure drag.||A textured ball can reduce total drag in suitable regimes despite altered skin-friction effects.||Total drag depends on speed, shape and flow conditions; one surface label cannot prove range.',
'The flow diagram illustrates mechanisms, not measured drag coefficients.','drag')
L(4,'bernoulli','Pressure differences and lift','Biomechanics',
'''Bernoulli’s principle relates pressure, speed and height along a streamline under suitable ideal flow assumptions. A faster local flow can correspond to lower static pressure in that model. Shape and orientation influence the surrounding flow and pressure distribution, which can produce a net force. This is not the claim that two air parcels must reunite after travelling around different sides.||Lift is a force perpendicular to relative flow and need not be upward in every situation. Drag acts along the relative-flow direction opposing motion. A ball or implement can experience pressure forces, viscous effects and wake behaviour together. A simplified pressure explanation should not exclude the role of momentum change in the surrounding fluid.||A sporting shape may generate a pressure difference that changes trajectory or support. The magnitude and direction depend on geometry, speed and conditions. An arrow diagram must state the relative flow and which region is higher or lower pressure. An unlabelled faster air slogan does not provide a complete physical account.||Use the principle as one part of a reasoned model and name its limits. Avoid equal-transit-time myths or assuming every curved surface guarantees useful upward lift. Real flows can be viscous, unsteady and separated. For a spinning ball, connect the orientation of spin and travel to the resulting force rather than using shape alone.''',
'Correct: air on the longer path must speed up to meet the air on the shorter path at the back.',
'There is no requirement for paired air parcels to meet again at the back.||Flow and pressure depend on shape, boundary conditions and interactions.||Bernoulli can relate local speed and pressure under suitable assumptions, but equal transit time is not the cause.',
'Why is lift not always upward? Distinguish it from drag in a stated relative-flow model.',
'Lift is defined perpendicular to relative flow, so its direction depends on orientation and pressure distribution.||Drag acts along the relative-flow direction opposing the object’s motion through the fluid.||State flow direction and geometry; pressure and momentum descriptions should be consistent.',
'The schematic does not model full viscous or separated airflow.','drag')
L(4,'magnus','Top spin, back spin and side spin','Biomechanics',
'''A spinning ball can experience an aerodynamic force that curves its path: the Magnus effect. Its direction depends on both travel and spin orientation. For a ball moving to the right in the diagram, top spin has the upper surface moving to the right relative to the centre; back spin has the upper surface moving left. In a common regime, top spin gives a downward force and back spin an upward force.||Side spin gives a lateral force whose direction depends on the axis and sense of rotation. A side-view drawing cannot establish every three-dimensional curve. State the viewing direction and spin before assigning left or right. Saying clockwise always curves left is meaningless without those references and the travel direction.||The effect involves flow asymmetry, pressure distribution and momentum change around the spinning ball. Real magnitude and even behaviour can depend on speed, surface, spin and flow regime. Gravity still acts, and drag can also alter speed; upward lift does not mean a back-spinning ball necessarily rises indefinitely.||Explain the expected path relative to an otherwise comparable non-spinning ball, then discuss a sporting use and limitation. A top-spinning tennis shot may dip more strongly, helping combine speed with court clearance in suitable conditions. The schematic illustrates physics, not the racket technique needed to produce the spin or an exact trajectory prediction.''',
'A ball travels right. In the stated diagram its upper surface rotates right for top spin and left for back spin. Predict common vertical Magnus directions.',
'Top spin: commonly downward Magnus force.||Back spin: commonly upward Magnus force.||Gravity and drag still act; real magnitude depends on speed, surface and spin. The prediction uses the stated viewing and travel directions.',
'Critique: clockwise spin always makes a ball curve left and back spin removes gravity.',
'Clockwise needs a viewing direction, and curve direction also needs travel and spin-axis information.||Back spin can create upward aerodynamic force in common conditions but does not remove gravity.||Resultant motion depends on all forces and conditions; the model is not a universal exact flight prediction.',
'Travel and spin orientation are labelled; force magnitudes are not to scale.','magnus')
L(4,'mental-strategies','Self-talk, relaxation, imagery and routines','Sport psychology',
'''Performance strategies aim to manage relevant attention, confidence and activation. Self-talk can use a concise task-focused cue rather than a prediction of guaranteed success. Relaxation strategies can help reduce unnecessary tension or activation in suitable contexts, while energising cues may be more useful when activation is too low.||A performance routine organises preparation into a familiar sequence. Goal setting can focus controllable actions and progress. Imagery mentally rehearses relevant aspects of performance, but it does not replace physical practice or guarantee the same result. Strategies before performance and during a rapid game phase face different time and attention constraints.||A free-throw routine might bring attention to a selected target and one action cue. During a rally, a brief reset cue may be more feasible than a long internal analysis. Individual preferences and task demands matter. A strategy that helps one athlete can distract another, so evaluate effects through discussion and repeated observations.||Explain the problem, strategy, mechanism and how its usefulness will be reviewed. Do not treat normal competition nerves as a clinical diagnosis or promise that a phrase eliminates anxiety. The site offers educational planning for sport performance; mental-health concerns require appropriate support outside an automated lesson score.''',
'A player becomes distracted after one error. Propose a brief during-performance strategy and explain its mechanism and limit.',
'A concise reset cue can redirect attention to the next relevant action.||A familiar routine or task-focused self-talk can reduce unproductive rumination and restore a controllable focus.||Effect varies; test with the athlete and teacher rather than guaranteeing removal of stress.',
'Compare imagery before performance and a brief cue during a fast rally. Why should relaxation not be prescribed identically to every athlete?',
'Pre-performance imagery can rehearse relevant action and decision sequences with more time available.||A rally needs a short cue that preserves attention to changing external information.||Some athletes need greater activation rather than calming; strategy depends on task and observed response.',
'Performance planning does not diagnose or treat mental-health conditions.','attention')
L(4,'cohesion','Task and social cohesion','Sport psychology',
'''Task cohesion concerns shared commitment to the group’s performance aims and coordinated work. Social cohesion concerns interpersonal connection and enjoyment of being part of the group. They can support each other but are not identical. A team can be friendly yet poorly organised, or task-focused without close friendships among every member.||Leadership, communication, shared goals and agreed roles can improve coordination. Team-building activities are useful when connected to actual needs rather than assumed to solve every conflict. Role clarity can help members understand what they contribute and how their actions relate to teammates’ responsibilities.||Cohesion changes over time and can be affected by success, conflict, fairness perceptions and team dynamics. A single win does not prove strong cohesion, and a loss does not prove it is absent. Effective communication should allow issues to be raised respectfully and expectations to be reviewed, not demand unquestioning agreement.||Analyse the stated problem: is it unclear task coordination, social exclusion or both? Select a strategy addressing that mechanism and identify evidence to monitor. A shared goal needs agreement and practical responsibilities; a slogan alone may not alter behaviour. Individual goals should align with team aims without erasing legitimate individual needs.''',
'A team enjoys social events but repeatedly duplicates defensive assignments. Identify the cohesion distinction and a targeted strategy.',
'Social cohesion may be strong while task cohesion or coordination remains weak.||Clarify roles, communication calls and shared defensive goals through an appropriate team review.||Monitor comparable game situations; more social events alone may not solve assignment confusion.',
'A team performs coordinated tactics but one member feels excluded. Explain why task success does not resolve the social issue.',
'Task cohesion and social cohesion are different dimensions.||Good tactical coordination does not prove everyone feels included.||Use respectful communication and appropriate leadership to address inclusion while maintaining clear performance roles; one result is not a full cohesion measure.',
'Cohesion is not measured reliably by winning alone.','cohesion')
L(4,'loafing','Social loafing and team accountability','Sport psychology',
'''Social loafing describes reduced individual effort when working in a group compared with an appropriate individual context. It can occur when contributions are hard to identify, responsibility is diffuse or a member feels their effort makes little difference. It should not be assigned from one mistake or a stereotype about a person.||Role clarity, meaningful individual responsibility and feedback can support engagement. Shared goals and recognition of useful contributions can help members understand why effort matters. Excessive public blame can damage trust and motivation, so accountability should use evidence and respectful expectations rather than humiliation.||Team dynamics include relationships, communication, conflict and leadership patterns. A low contribution might also reflect unclear instruction, fatigue, injury or an inappropriate role; these are different explanations from social loafing. A coach should investigate context before treating all reduced output as unwillingness.||In a case response, describe the behaviour, propose plausible causes and justify an intervention. Use observation across comparable situations and conversation with members to test the explanation. A successful strategy should strengthen both responsibility and coordinated support. The site’s fictional cases do not classify real classmates or evaluate their character.''',
'In a fictional group drill, responsibilities are unclear and some members reduce effort. Explain possible social loafing and one alternative cause.',
'Diffuse responsibility and invisible contribution can support social loafing.||Unclear instructions may also cause low contribution without deliberate effort reduction.||Clarify meaningful roles and feedback, then observe comparable tasks and discuss needs rather than blame from one example.',
'Evaluate public criticism as the sole response to low group effort. Propose a more evidence-based accountability strategy.',
'Public blame can damage trust and may misidentify the cause.||Establish clear roles, agreed expectations and feedback on observable contributions.||Investigate alternative explanations and monitor changes; accountability should support useful effort and team coordination.',
'The lesson does not label real students from a single behaviour.','cohesion')
L(4,'written-exam','Written exam design and original rehearsal','Integrated application',
'''The current Year 12 written examination allows ten minutes reading and two and a half hours working. Section One has twenty multiple-choice questions worth twenty per cent, with a suggested thirty minutes. Section Two has six to ten short-answer questions worth fifty per cent, with a suggested seventy minutes. Section Three requires two of four extended-answer questions worth thirty per cent, with a suggested fifty minutes.||Read the actual paper instructions because syllabus and examination versions can change. The written component and practical component are separate: the combined Year 12 assessment weighting is seventy per cent written and thirty per cent practical. The written school-assessment types have their own weights, which must not be confused with the exam’s section percentages.||Explain questions need a connected mechanism, not just a named term. Analysis uses the supplied data and identifies relationships. Evaluation makes a judgement using criteria and recognises a limit. Numerical work should show formula, substitution, units and a contextual conclusion. A diagram needs labels and explanatory notes connected to the answer.||This course includes original practice and suggested points, not an official SCSA paper or key. A self-review score and concept mastery cannot predict a grade. Use official papers alongside school tasks and timed practice. Practical skill and tactical execution require the school’s teacher-managed programme; completing a browser lesson is not completion of the practical assessment.''',
'A question asks why an athlete spins faster after reducing moment of inertia. Write an explanation with the necessary assumption.',
'With negligible net external torque, angular momentum L = Iω is conserved.||Reducing I requires ω to increase to maintain L.||The athlete spins faster without creating extra angular momentum; if external torque is significant the simple conservation account needs revision.',
'A question asks whether a fictional training intervention caused improvement when conditions and opponents also changed. Write an evaluative conclusion.',
'The observed improvement is compatible with benefit but does not isolate the intervention as the cause.||Changed conditions and opposition are confounding factors.||Use comparable repeated observations or a suitable design before a stronger causal claim; identify what the data support and what remains uncertain.',
'Practice points are authored suggestions, not official marking or ATAR prediction.','exam')
